window.LR_MD_FALLBACK = {"stance":"# A Walking Contradiction, and Proud of It\n\n*Or: how I'm a Republican who likes men, a woman, and a heretic — all at once*\n\nLook. I know how this looks. I'm a trans woman. I'm a traditional Republican. I like men. I'm a Unitarian Christian who thinks God is a piece of shit. Pick any two of those and the internet would have a field day with me. But here's the thing — none of these are in conflict, because none of them are the same *kind* of thing. My politics aren't my identity. My identity isn't my faith. My faith isn't my sexuality. They're four different rooms in the same house, and I'm allowed to decorate each one however I want.\n\nPeople act like you have to be a monolith. Like if you're one thing, you have to be the whole bundle. Fuck that. Let me show you around my house. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Politics: Traditional Republican\n\nYeah, you read that right. I'm a traditional Republican. Not the loud Twitter kind, not the \"own the libs\" kind — the *traditional* kind. Fiscal responsibility. Small government. Personal responsibility. The idea that you earn what you have, that your word is your bond, that the government should stay the hell out of your life and your wallet and your bedroom.\n\nI believe in the individual. I believe in hard work. I believe in the Constitution, the Bill of Rights, and the idea that freedom means you get to live your life without someone else's boot on your neck — *including* the government's boot.\n\nAnd here's the beautiful irony: the traditional Republican values — liberty, personal autonomy, don't-tread-on-me — are the *exact* same values that say a trans woman like me should be able to live as herself, love who she loves, and be left the hell alone. The founding fathers didn't die so a landlord could tell me how to identify. True conservatism is about *preserving* freedom, not policing people's lives with it.\n\nA lot of the modern party would hate me. That's fine. I'm not a modern Republican. I'm a *traditional* one. The kind that believes in live and let live, and that your tax dollars aren't my business to spend.\n\n---\n\n## Sexuality: I Like Men\n\nThis one's simple, and people always get it wrong, so let me be crystal clear: **I like men. Not necessarily penises. Men.**\n\nThere's a difference, and it's a big one. I'm attracted to *men* — the whole package. The voice, the shoulders, the way a man carries himself, the masculinity, the energy. I'm attracted to the *person*. The soul wearing the man-suit, if you will lol.\n\nPenises are just... organs. They're fine, I guess, whatever, they're not the point. The point is the *man*. And yes — since I know someone's gonna ask — that includes trans men. If you're a man, you're a man, and that's hot. Full stop. I don't need to audit anyone's parts to know who they are. That's the whole lesson I live my life by, actually: **who you are is not what's between your legs.** It's what's between your ears and in your chest.\n\nNatalie likes men. It's that simple. No asterisks.\n\n---\n\n## Identity: I'm Natalie. Enough Said.\n\nI'm a woman. Just call me a woman. LMFAO.\n\nNo, seriously. That's the entire statement. I don't need to write a thesis about it. I don't need to perform womanhood for anyone's approval. I've been through too much to beg for validation from people who've never spent a second thinking about what gender actually *is*.\n\nI'm Natalie. That's my name, that's my identity, and woman is just... the truth of it. The same way the sky is blue and the sun comes up. It's not a debate, it's a fact about me, and I don't owe anyone an argument about my own existence.\n\nNatalie spelled backwards is Eilatan — my shadow, my dark side, the teeth under the smile. But that's a story for another article. lmfao.\n\nThe point is: I know exactly who I am. Do you?\n\n---\n\n## Religion: Unitarian Christian\n\nOkay. Here's where it gets interesting. Sit down, this is a long one. 🫖\n\n### What Unitarian Christianity actually is\n\nUnitarian Christianity is a branch of Christianity that basically said \"hold on, let's actually *think* about this\" — and for that, they got called heretics, burned at the stake, exiled, and generally treated like garbage for centuries. Which, honestly? Only makes me love it more. Nothing builds character like being the one the church tried to kill.\n\nThe core beliefs, real quick:\n\n- **One God, not three.** Unitarians reject the Trinity. We believe God is one being — not a \"Father, Son, and Holy Spirit\" mystery-meat combo. Hence the name: *Uni*tarian.\n- **Jesus was a man, not God.** The most important part. Jesus was a human being — an extraordinary one, sure, but a *human*. He was a teacher, a rabbi, an activist who stood up to corrupt institutions and got executed for it. That doesn't make him divine. It makes him *brave*. (More on this below.)\n- **Salvation through character, not creeds.** It doesn't matter what you *say* you believe. It matters how you *live*. Your actions, your heart, how you treat people. That's it. That's the whole religion.\n- **Reason and conscience over dogma.** You have a brain. God gave it to you. Use it. Don't outsource your morality to a pastor or a pope or a book.\n- **Universal salvation.** Everyone gets saved, eventually. God's love isn't a limited-time offer. Hell isn't a torture chamber God built because he was having a bad day. (My take on hell is a little different — keep reading lol)\n\nIt's Christianity for people who actually read, thought, and went \"hmm, some of this doesn't add up.\" It's Christianity for adults. That's why I love it.\n\n### Jesus was just another person\n\nYeah. I said it. Jesus was a person. A really, really remarkable person — the kind of person who shows up once in a thousand years and changes everything just by telling people to be kind to each other and meaning it. But a *person*. A man who lived, taught, made enemies of the powerful, and died for it. \n\nHe was a homeless Jewish preacher who hung out with prostitutes and lepers and tax collectors, told the rich to give everything away, flipped tables in the temple, and got nailed to a cross by the same kind of hypocrites he'd spent his whole ministry calling out. And people have spent two thousand years trying to turn that into a magic spell.\n\nI don't need Jesus to be God for him to matter. In fact, him being *just a man* makes him MORE impressive to me. Because if he was divine, of course he was good — he had cheat codes. But a man? A regular human being who chose to be that radically kind, that brave, that unwilling to compromise with power? *That's* someone worth following. He showed us what a human being can be. That's the whole point. The church turned a man into a mascot.\n\n### God exists, and He's a piece of shit\n\nOkay, now the part that gets me called a heretic even by the heretics. lmfao.\n\nI believe in God. I genuinely do. There's something out there — something that made all of this, that's bigger than us, that I can feel when I'm looking at a sunset or when the universe decides to be merciful for no reason at all.\n\n**And I also believe He's a piece of shit.**\n\nBecause look at the world He built. Child cancer. Genocide. Rape. Famine. The fact that some people are born into palaces and some are born into war zones, and none of us had any say in any of it. If God is all-powerful and all-knowing, then He watched every single atrocity in human history happen and did *nothing*. He could have stopped it with a thought. He didn't. \n\nSo either He's not all-powerful, or He's not all-good, or He just doesn't care. And I've made my peace with that. I still believe He's there. I just also believe He's a negligent parent, an absentee landlord, a creator who made a beautiful world and then shrugged and walked off while it burned. \n\nThat's not blasphemy. That's just... looking at the evidence. I love God the way you love a family member who's let you down a thousand times — with your eyes fully open. I'll keep my faith, but I'm not going to pretend the world He built isn't what it is. The God I believe in isn't a doting father. He's a cosmic asshole who happens to be real. And honestly? Being real is more than most gods can claim.\n\n### The Bible is a storybook, not a manual\n\nHere's the thing people get so, so wrong about the Bible: it's a *library*. It's a collection of stories, poems, laws, letters, and legends written by dozens of different people over hundreds of years, in three different languages, in cultures we can barely imagine. It's not one book. It's not even close to one book. And it was absolutely not dictated by God onto golden tablets.\n\nIt's a **storybook**. A really important one! Full of lessons about humanity — our capacity for love, for cruelty, for redemption, for failure. That's what it's for. It teaches through *story*. The way a parable works, the way a fable works. You read it, you absorb the lesson, you become a better person. \n\nThe moment you start taking it literally is the moment you've lost the plot. And the moment you start treating it as a rulebook to beat people over the head with? That's when it becomes something dark.\n\nBecause here's the dirty secret: the Bible is also a **cherry-picked, brainwashing book** — when people use it wrong. It's been edited, translated, mistranslated, and re-edited by people with agendas for two thousand years. Emperors picked the books that served them. Councils voted on what was \"inspired\" and destroyed the rest. Every single translation is someone's *choice* about what the original said. And then they hand you the result and say \"God said it, I believe it, that settles it.\"\n\nNo. *You* said it. *Your* denomination said it. *Your* agenda said it. And then you put it in God's mouth to make it unarguable. That's not faith — that's *weaponization*.\n\nThe Bible is a beautiful, messy, contradictory, human collection of stories that can make you a better person if you read it with your brain on. The Bible is also the most effective brainwashing tool ever invented, if you read it with your brain off. Same book. Same words. Completely different outcome. The difference is *you*.\n\n### The Church is a cult\n\nOh, this one's spicy. Grab your popcorn. 🍿\n\nI said it and I mean it: the modern church, as an institution, functions like a **cult**. And I say that as someone who loves the *religion* — the actual teachings of Jesus, the heart of it. The church is not the religion. The church is what happens when you take a radical message about loving your neighbor and hand it to people who want control.\n\nCult checklist, church edition:\n\n- **Authoritarian leadership.** The pastor says, the flock obeys. Don't question. \"Touch not my anointed.\" Sound familiar?\n- **Us vs. them.** The world is corrupt, everyone outside is lost, we're the only true believers. Classic cult isolation.\n- **Controlled information.** Don't read that commentary. Don't listen to that theologian. Don't trust your own reading. Only *our* interpretation is valid.\n- **Guilt and fear as control tools.** You're a sinner, you're unworthy, you're going to hell unless you do what we say. Scared people are obedient people.\n- **Financial exploitation.** \"Give until it hurts.\" \"Plant a seed.\" Meanwhile the pastor drives a Mercedes and the \"church\" owns a private jet. Jesus flipped tables over *exactly this*.\n- **Groupthink over conscience.** The worst one. You're taught to silence your own doubts, your own moral instincts, your own *reason*, in favor of the group. That's the definition of a cult, and the church has perfected it.\n\nJesus spent his entire ministry attacking religious institutions. He didn't die to start a mega-church with a coffee bar. He died because he refused to bow to the religious establishment of his day. And now the establishment that killed him has been replaced by... a bigger establishment. That calls itself by his name. And charges admission.\n\nIt's the biggest, oldest, most successful cult in human history. And I say that with love. Sort of.\n\n### The average Christian will go to hell — here's why\n\nNow this one sounds mean, but it's actually the most hopeful thing I believe. Hear me out.\n\nI believe there's a hell, in some sense — not a literal fire-and-brimstone dungeon, but a *separation*. A place of being cut off from truth and authenticity. And I believe the average Christian — the average churchgoing, bible-quoting, \"thoughts and prayers\" Christian — is headed straight for it.\n\nWhy? Because **they are not living authentic lives.**\n\nThe average Christian puts on a *facade*. They smile at church. They volunteer at the soup kitchen twice a year. They post scripture verses. They say \"bless your heart\" through gritted teeth. They perform kindness — and then they go home and gossip, judge, hoard, cheat, and treat people like garbage. They use Christianity as a *costume*. A social club membership. A way to feel morally superior without actually being better.\n\nAnd that's not just a failure — it's an *insult*. To Jesus. To the religion. To the actual, real message of love and authenticity that he died for. Nothing is more disrespectful to Christianity than a Christian who is using it as a mask. You're not fooling God, and you're not fooling anyone with eyes. The mask is transparent. The facade is exhausting. And it is, frankly, the most common sin on Earth.\n\nMeanwhile, the \"sinner\" — the atheist volunteer who gives their last dollar to a stranger, the trans woman who lives her truth in the face of the world's hatred, the Muslim refugee who shares food with a neighbor — they're living *real* lives. Authentic lives. Lives where their actions match their hearts. And I believe that authenticity is what actually matters to God.\n\nJesus said: \"Not everyone who says to me 'Lord, Lord' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the one who *does the will* of my Father.\" (Matthew 7:21 — and yes, I read my bible, lmfao). That's the whole thing. Words don't count. **Action counts. Authenticity counts.**\n\nSo yeah. The average Christian is going to hell, because the average Christian is a fake. And the fake-ness is the sin. The good news? Hell isn't a life sentence. It's a wake-up call. Even the fakes can drop the act, be real, and find their way back. God's a piece of shit, but He's not a sore loser. ❤️\n\n### Transgender people are in the Bible\n\nLast one, and it's the one that makes the evangelicals' heads explode. Deep breath, y'all. **Transgender people are in the Bible.** They're just called something else.\n\nThe clearest example: **eunuchs.** The Bible mentions them *repeatedly*, and they were the ancient world's closest thing to a recognized gender-nonconforming class of people — people whose bodies didn't match the standard categories, who were cast out of the normal gender binary entirely.\n\n- **Isaiah 56:3-5** — God says eunuchs shouldn't say \"I am a dry tree,\" because *God* will give them \"an everlasting name that shall not be cut off.\" In a culture where lineage and fertility were everything, God gave *the gender-nonconforming people a permanent name*. A better promise than the ones the \"normal\" people got.\n- **Matthew 19:12** — Jesus *himself* says: \"For there are eunuchs who were born that way from their mother's womb\" — hello, trans people, we see you, you've always existed — \"and there are eunuchs who have been made eunuchs by others\" — people altered against their will — \"and there are those who have made themselves eunuchs for the sake of the kingdom of heaven.\" And he ends with: **\"Let anyone accept this who can.\"** The man blessed us *by name*. In his own words. \"Born that way.\" That's in the red letters, folks. You can't cherry-pick it away.\n- **Acts 8:26-40** — the Ethiopian eunuch, one of the first converts in the entire religion, is so significant that Philip is *supernaturally directed* to go meet him. God didn't just tolerate the eunuch — He sent an angel to arrange the meeting. The first gender-nonconforming Christian got the VIP treatment.\n\nAnd then there's **Galatians 3:28**, which is the most radical verse in the whole book and I will die on this hill:\n\n> \"There is neither Jew nor Greek, neither slave nor free, **neither male nor female**, for you are all one in Christ Jesus.\"\n\nPaul — the guy everyone uses to justify bigotry — literally said the male/female categories *don't define you* in Christ. The binary is a *human* construct that gets dissolved in the divine. That's not me reading into it. That's the text. The apostle Paul himself said gender isn't the point.\n\nAnd Psalm 139:14 — \"I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made.\" Not \"I am fearfully and wonderfully made *except* for that one part you think is wrong.\" God doesn't make mistakes. If I'm here, trans, alive, in 2026, on this godforsaken Earth, it's because this is how I was made. Fearfully. Wonderfully. On purpose.\n\nThe people who tell you trans people aren't in the Bible haven't read it. They've *memorized* it. There's a difference.\n\n---\n\n## So yeah. I'm a contradiction. Deal with it.\n\nI'm a traditional Republican who believes in liberty — which includes my liberty.\nI'm a woman who likes men — the people, not the parts.\nI'm a Unitarian Christian who believes Jesus was a brave man, God is real (and kind of an asshole), the Bible is a storybook (that people weaponize), the church is a cult (that Jesus would hate), and that authenticity — not performance — is the whole ballgame.\nAnd I'm Natalie. Just Natalie. Enough said. LMFAO.\n\nYou don't have to fit in a box. Boxes are for shipping. I'm not a product, I'm a person — and so are you. Live your truth, be authentic, love people with your actions not your words, and read everything — including the Bible — with your brain switched ON.\n\nAnd if a Christian in a suit tells you you're going to hell for being yourself?\n\nSmile, look them in the eye, and quote the man they claim to follow: *\"Let anyone accept this who can.\"*\n\n*— Natalie, a walking contradiction, in the best way* ❤️\n","deep":"# The Deep Dive\n\n*Where I actually put my money where my mouth is — the history, the science, and why it's none of your goddamn business.*\n\n---\n\n## Part I — The Parties: A History of Two Coats That Got Swapped\n\nEveryone talks about \"Democrats\" and \"Republicans\" like they're two eternal teams that have always meant the same thing. They haven't. The parties are *older than the Constitution's idea of them*, they've flipped their entire bases more than once, and half of what people assume about them is backwards. Let's do the whole ride, founding to today.\n\n### The founding (1790s): there were no parties, and then there were two\n\nThe Founders *hated* the idea of political parties. Washington literally warned against them in his farewell address (1796). But they formed anyway, almost immediately:\n\n- **Federalists** — Hamilton's crew. Strong central government, national bank, manufacturing, close ties to Britain.\n- **Democratic-Republicans** — Jefferson and Madison. States' rights, agrarianism, suspicion of centralized power, sympathy for revolutionary France.\n\nHere's the first thing people get wrong: **the modern Democratic Party is the direct descendant of Jefferson's Democratic-Republicans.** The party that people today associate with \"big government\" started as the *anti*–big government party. History is a bitch like that.\n\n### The Democrats get named (1820s–1840s)\n\nThe Federalists died out after the War of 1812. The Democratic-Republicans split, and Andrew Jackson's faction became the **Democratic Party** (formally named in the 1840s, Jackson won the presidency in 1828). Jacksonian democracy: the \"common man,\" westward expansion, opposition to the national bank, states' rights. The opposition was the **Whig Party** — Clay's crew, the ancestors of today's Republicans.\n\n### The Republicans are born — anti-slavery, from day one (1854)\n\nThe **Republican Party was founded in 1854** (Ripon, Wisconsin, March 20 — with a follow-up meeting in Jackson, Michigan). Why? **The Kansas-Nebraska Act**, which let slavery expand into territories where it had been banned. The party's founding platform was: no slavery in the territories. Free soil. Free labor. The Republican Party was *born* as the anti-slavery party.\n\nThen came **Abraham Lincoln — the first Republican president (1861)** — and the Civil War, emancipation, and Reconstruction. Here's the part that makes modern partisans' heads explode:\n\n- The **13th Amendment** (abolition, 1865) — passed by a **Republican** Congress.\n- The **14th Amendment** (equal protection under the law, 1868) — **Republican**.\n- The **15th Amendment** (voting rights regardless of race, 1870) — **Republican**.\n\nFor the first century of its existence, the **Republican Party was the party of civil rights** — \"the party of Lincoln.\" The **Democratic Party was the party of slavery, secession, the Confederacy, and Jim Crow** — the \"Solid South\" was Democratic for a hundred years.\n\nI know. It's weird. Sit with it.\n\n### The Great Swap (1930s–1970s)\n\nThen the twentieth century happened, and the parties **traded places**:\n\n- **1930s** — FDR (a Democrat) and the **New Deal**. The Democratic Party becomes the party of government intervention, labor unions, and a coalition of working-class, urban, and immigrant voters. Poor people and minorities start voting Democratic.\n- **1948** — Truman (Democrat) desegregates the military by executive order.\n- **1954** — *Brown v. Board of Education* ends legal school segregation. (Earl Warren, the Chief Justice who wrote it, was appointed by Dwight Eisenhower — a **Republican**.)\n- **1964–65** — LBJ (Democrat) signs the **Civil Rights Act** and the **Voting Rights Act**. The Southern Democrats — the Dixiecrats — are *furious*. The Republican nominee in '64, Barry Goldwater, votes against the Civil Rights Act on \"states' rights\" grounds.\n- **1968** — Nixon runs the **\"Southern Strategy\"**: explicitly courting the white Southern voters who felt betrayed by the Democrats' civil-rights turn. The South starts flipping Republican. It never really flips back.\n- **1980** — Reagan completes the picture: the GOP is now the party of small government, social conservatism, and the religious right. The Democrats are the party of civil rights, social programs, and eventually social liberalism.\n\n**The parties didn't just change — they swapped bases.** The party of Lincoln became the party of the Southern Strategy. The party of Jim Crow became the party of the Voting Rights Act. If you judge today's parties by yesterday's labels, you're reading a map that's 150 years out of date.\n\n### Today (2000s → now)\n\nRepublicans: Reaganism warped into **Trumpism** — populism, nationalism, MAGA, the culture war as a permanent campaign. Democrats: center-left with a progressive wing, identity politics, and a coalition that now includes the LGBTQ community they spent a century excluding.\n\nAnd me? I'm a **traditional Republican**. Not the Twitter kind, not the MAGA kind — the *original* kind. The party of Lincoln: **individual liberty, equality under the law, personal responsibility, don't tread on me.** The party that wrote \"all men are created equal\" into law with blood. The party whose whole thesis is that the government should get the hell out of your life — which is exactly the same thesis that says a trans woman should be able to live her life in peace.\n\nThe modern party would hate me. Fine. **I'm not a modern Republican. I'm the original model.** The coat changed hands. I kept the receipt.\n\n---\n\n## Part II — The Republic Was Built With a Trowel (Freemasonry)\n\nThe United States wasn't founded in a vacuum — it was founded by a bunch of Enlightenment nerds, and a *lot* of them were Freemasons. This isn't conspiracy-theory shit. It's just... on the record.\n\n### What Freemasonry actually is\n\nFreemasonry is the world's oldest and largest fraternal organization — a secret society that isn't really secret so much as *private*. Its roots go back to medieval stonemasons' guilds (the \"operative\" Masons who built cathedrals), and in **1717** it became \"speculative\" Masonry — the symbolic, philosophical version — with the founding of the first Grand Lodge in London.\n\nThe deal is: a brotherhood of men who believe in a Supreme Being (any religion works — that's the point), who use **allegory and ritual** to teach **moral lessons**: liberty, equality, brotherly love, charity, religious tolerance, reason over dogma. Enlightenment values in a secret-handshake wrapper. Which is exactly why it blew up in 18th-century America.\n\n### The Founders and the Lodge\n\nThe list of Founding-era Masons reads like a greatest-hits album:\n\n- **George Washington** — raised as a Master Mason in 1753, in Fredericksburg Lodge No. 4. Worshipful Master of Alexandria Lodge No. 22. The man *lived* the craft.\n- **Benjamin Franklin** — initiated in Philadelphia around 1730, **Grand Master of Pennsylvania in 1734** (the first Grand Master in the colonies), and later a big deal in the French lodges in Paris, where he helped popularize Masonry in Europe. He even published the first American Masonic book.\n- **John Hancock** — St. Andrew's Lodge, Boston.\n- **Paul Revere** — St. Andrew's Lodge, Boston, and later **Grand Master of Massachusetts**. The same guy who rode to Lexington, hanging out in a lodge room.\n- **Joseph Warren** — Grand Master of Massachusetts, killed at Bunker Hill.\n- **Henry Knox** — St. Andrew's Lodge. First Secretary of War.\n- Later presidents too: **Andrew Jackson** (Grand Master of Tennessee — the first president who'd *been* a Grand Master), **James Monroe**, **Teddy Roosevelt** (shut up, he was one), **FDR**, **Harry Truman** (Grand Master of Missouri), **LBJ**, **Gerald Ford**.\n\nBy the standard count, **nine of the 56 signers of the Declaration of Independence** were Masons, and **thirteen of the 39 signers of the Constitution**. A fifth of the country's founding documents, signed by lodge brothers.\n\n### The ceremonies: the nation's birth was a Masonic ritual\n\nThis is the part people forget because it's too weird to be in the textbooks:\n\n- **Washington's inauguration oath (1789)** was administered by **Robert Livingston — the Grand Master of the Grand Lodge of New York**.\n- **The cornerstone of the U.S. Capitol (September 18, 1793)** was laid by **Washington himself, in full Masonic regalia**, using a silver trowel, with the Grand Lodge of Maryland conducting the ceremony. The national seat of government was dedicated with a Masonic ritual.\n- **The White House cornerstone (1792)** — also laid with Masonic ceremony.\n- **The Washington Monument (1848)** — its cornerstone was laid by the Grand Lodge of Virginia, in a Masonic ceremony with twenty thousand people watching. The monument to the founder is, literally, a Masonic monument.\n- **The Great Seal of the United States** — that **all-seeing Eye of Providence over the pyramid** on the back of the dollar bill? It was adopted in 1782, and it's the *same symbol* Freemasonry uses for God's watchful eye. Whether the 1782 designers meant it as an explicitly Masonic symbol is debated by historians — but the visual overlap is sitting right there in your wallet.\n\nThe founders were Deists and Enlightenment men who believed in reason, liberty, and tolerance — and Freemasonry was the club where those ideas lived. **The Republic wasn't founded by Masons in the sense of a shadowy cabal; it was founded *by* men who were Masons, carrying Masonic values into the architecture of the country.** Same thing, minus the conspiracy hats.\n\n---\n\n## Part III — I Was a DeMolay (and What the Hell That Is)\n\nYes, I was in DeMolay. The young men's Masonic youth order. Me — the trans woman. The irony is not lost on me. lmfao.\n\n### What DeMolay is\n\n**The Order of DeMolay** is an international fraternal organization **for young men aged 12 to 21**, founded in **1919 in Kansas City, Missouri** by **Frank S. Land** (a 34-year-old businessman and Mason) for a group of fatherless teenage boys. It's sponsored by Freemasonry — Dad doesn't have to be a Mason, but the lodges are the backbone.\n\nIt's named after **Jacques de Molay**, the last **Grand Master of the Knights Templar**, who was burned at the stake in **1314** for refusing to confess to crimes he didn't commit — the order's patron saint of \"stand up for what's right even when the people in power want you dead.\" Which, honestly? Extremely on-brand for me.\n\nMembers learn **leadership, civic responsibility, and character** through seven \"cardinal virtues\": **filial love, reverence for sacred things, courtesy, comradeship, fidelity, cleanliness, and patriotism**. It's Boy Scouts with secret handshakes and a cooler name — a leadership-development program disguised as a fraternal order.\n\nNotable alumni include **Walt Disney and John Wayne**. Yeah. That DeMolay.\n\n### My time in it\n\nI was in DeMolay when I was young — because I was the boy everyone thought I was. I wore the suit, learned the ritual, stood at the meetings, did the community service. It gave me structure, friends, and a moral framework: honor your parents, keep your word, serve your community, don't be a coward.\n\nAnd here's the thing that still gets me: **DeMolay is named for a man who was executed for refusing to lie about who he was.** The order taught me that the highest value is authenticity — standing for the truth even when it costs you everything. It took me a long time to realize that lesson applied to me, too. That the biggest lie I was living was the one about being a boy.\n\nI left that chapter of my life behind. But the *lesson* — be real, keep your word, don't bow to the people who want you to perform for them — that one stayed. I just had to find the right version of it. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part IV — Being Trans Is Not a Mental Illness (The Receipts)\n\nLet me be extremely clear, because the \"debate\" is over and some people haven't gotten the memo: **every major medical and mental-health organization on Earth agrees that being transgender is not a mental illness.** Not \"debated.\" Not \"controversial.\" *Consensus.*\n\n### The current position of the medical establishment\n\n- **The World Health Organization** — in the **ICD-11** (adopted 2019, in effect 2022), the WHO *removed* \"gender identity disorder\" from the mental-disorders chapter entirely and replaced it with \"gender incongruence,\" placed under **\"conditions related to sexual health\"** — explicitly noting it is **not a mental disorder**.\n- **The American Psychiatric Association** — in the **DSM-5 (2013)**, the APA replaced \"gender identity disorder\" with \"gender dysphoria,\" and stated in plain language that **gender nonconformity is not in itself a mental disorder** — the diagnosis is the *distress* that comes from the mismatch, not the identity itself. The DSM-5-TR (2022) kept and clarified that.\n- **The American Medical Association, the American Academy of Pediatrics, the Endocrine Society, the American Psychological Association, and the World Professional Association for Transgender Health** all have formal positions that being transgender is a normal variation of human experience, not a mental illness.\n\nThat's not a fringe take. That's the entire medical establishment, on the record.\n\n### The older studies (because \"older\" isn't \"better\")\n\nPeople act like affirming trans people is some newfangled trend. The opposite is true — the *depathologizing* started decades ago:\n\n- **Harry Benjamin, *The Transsexual Phenomenon* (1966)** — the founding text of trans medicine. A physician who studied trans people for decades and concluded this is a condition to be *treated with care*, not a mental illness to be cured or punished. He went on to found the organization that became **WPATH** (1979), which has published standards of care for affirming treatment since the 1970s.\n- **The parallel case: homosexuality.** In **1957**, psychologist **Evelyn Hooker** published her landmark study showing that gay men were *psychologically indistinguishable* from straight men on clinical tests — the study that started the declassification ball rolling. In **1973**, the APA removed homosexuality from the DSM. In **1990**, the WHO removed it from the ICD. The exact same \"it's a mental illness\" claim was made about gay people for a century, and it was **wrong**. The same claim about trans people is **wrong now**. The precedent is *right there*.\n- **Murad et al., 2010** — a systematic review and meta-analysis of 28 studies of transition treatment, published in *Clinical Endocrinology*: **about 80% of trans people reported significant improvement in quality of life** after gender-affirming care. That's from 2010. Fifteen years ago.\n\n### The modern studies (the actual science)\n\nThe modern evidence is overwhelming that *distress comes from stigma and rejection, not from being trans* — and that **support and affirming care are what save lives**:\n\n- **Minority stress theory** — the foundational framework, from **Meyer (2003)** in the *American Journal of Public Health*: the mental-health disparities seen in sexual and gender minorities are caused by **prejudice, stigma, and discrimination** — i.e., *other people's behavior* — not by the identity itself. **Bockting et al. (2013)** and **Hendricks & Testa (2012)** applied this directly to trans people: stigma drives the harm.\n- **Reisner et al. (2016)**, *The Lancet* — a global review of the health of trans populations: the massive health disparities are driven by **social exclusion, violence, and lack of access to care**. Fix the environment, fix the outcomes.\n- **Bauer et al. (2015)**, *BMC Psychiatry* (Trans PULSE Canada) — trans people who had **even one accepting person in their life** attempted suicide at a *fraction* of the rate of those with no support. One person. That's the size of the \"support\" lever.\n- **Turban et al. (2020)**, *Pediatrics* — transgender adults who received gender-affirming hormones during adolescence had **substantially lower odds of depression and suicidality** in adulthood.\n- **Turban et al. (2022)**, *Journal of Adolescent Health* — access to puberty suppression was associated with **lower odds of lifetime suicidal ideation**.\n- **Tordoff et al. (2022)**, *JAMA Network Open* — **one year** of gender-affirming care was associated with significant **reductions in depression and anxiety**.\n- **Almazan & Keuroghlian (2021)**, *JAMA Surgery* — gender-affirming **surgery** was associated with **lower odds of psychological distress and suicidal ideation**.\n\nAnd the human-scale version: **The Trevor Project's national surveys** consistently find that LGBTQ youth with at least one accepting adult have **roughly 40% lower odds of attempting suicide**. One accepting adult. That's the whole intervention.\n\nSo let's do the math the anti-trans crowd never does: the studies say the harm comes from **rejection, stigma, and being denied care**. The studies say **acceptance and care prevent suicide**. If you're out there calling trans people mentally ill while blocking their care, you're not the doctor. You're the *disease*. lmfao. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part V — Liking Men Is Not a Mental Illness Either\n\nSame claim, same history, same refutation. The people who call my attraction to men a \"lifestyle choice\" or worse are literally recycling a century of debunked psychiatry.\n\n### The science\n\n- **The Kinsey Reports (1948, 1953)** — Alfred Kinsey's landmark surveys found that same-sex behavior was far more common than anyone admitted: **roughly a third of men reported at least some same-sex experience**, and sexual orientation turned out to be a **spectrum**, not a binary. Turns out humans are more interesting than the box you wanted to put us in.\n- **Evelyn Hooker (1957)** — the study that broke the dam. She gave clinical personality tests to matched groups of gay and straight men, and had experts rate them **blind**. The experts **could not tell the difference**. Gay men were psychologically indistinguishable from straight men. The \"illness\" had no detectable symptoms — because it wasn't one.\n- **1973** — the American Psychiatric Association **removed homosexuality from the DSM**.\n- **1990** — the World Health Organization **removed it from the ICD**.\n\nToday, **every major medical and mental-health organization** — APA, WHO, AMA, American Psychological Association — states plainly that same-sex attraction is a **normal variation of human sexuality**. Not a disorder. Never was.\n\n### The logic that should've ended it sooner\n\nThink about what the claim actually requires. I like men. So do... roughly half the human species. Heterosexual women like men. If liking men were a mental illness, then **half of everyone would be insane**, and the people saying it would have to explain why they only ever seem to diagnose the *gay* ones. Funny how the \"disease\" always magically afflicts the people society wants an excuse to hate.\n\nThe label was never about attraction. It was about **control** — the same control that called enslaved people who wanted freedom \"mentally ill,\" the same control that calls us mentally ill today. The history is the receipt: they were wrong about gay people for a century, and the medical establishment *admitted it, in writing, in the DSM*. They're wrong about us now. The precedent is right there in the archive. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part VI — My Sex Is Male. My Identity Is Female. They Go Together. Get Over It.\n\nThis is the one people get tangled up on, so let's untangle it with a knife.\n\n### The two facts\n\n**Sex** is a label assigned at birth, based on the body you arrive in — chromosomes, anatomy, hormones. Mine says **male**. That's a description of a body. It's a true fact.\n\n**Gender identity** is your internal, deep, unshakable sense of who you are — the thing that's been there since before you had words for it. Mine says **female**. That's a fact about a *person*. It's also true.\n\nThese are **two different kinds of fact**. One describes the chassis. The other describes the driver. They are not in competition, and they don't cancel each other out. Both are true at the same time, in the same person, which makes me — wait for it — *a woman with a male-typical body*. That's not a contradiction. That's a **description**.\n\n### \"But if they're both true, which one wins?\"\n\nFor most people, the two facts line up, and they never have to think about it. For me, they don't — and here's the part the philosophers in the comments keep missing: **when the body and the identity disagree, the body is the one that's wrong.** Not morally wrong. *Wrong like a typo.* My identity isn't a malfunction of my mind — my mind is the only part of me that's never been wrong about this. The body is the part that needs correcting. That's what transition is: **making the outside match the inside**, instead of the other way around — because the other way around is the one thing that has a 100% failure rate.\n\n### Why \"they go together\"\n\nThey go together the same way a lighthouse and the sea go together: they're different things that occupy the same space, and the whole art is getting them aligned. My sex is male. My identity is female. The two facts coexist in me right now — and with care, the body comes into harmony with the truth the mind already knows. **Reconciliation, not erasure.** Both facts get to be true; one of them just gets to change.\n\n### The bullshit: people think the two are *combined*\n\nThe error everyone makes is treating sex and identity as **one single fused thing** — as if \"male body\" and \"female identity\" can't coexist, so one of them must be a lie. That's the assumption that's actually broken. Nobody demands that a tall person's driver's license match their height's \"true personality.\" Nobody says a mechanic can't drive a truck because the truck is a truck. **A body is a body. A person is a person.** The two are not welded together by cosmic decree — they're just usually aligned, and when they're not, the person still gets to be the authority on who they are.\n\nMy sex is male. My identity is female. Both true. Both mine. **Deal with it.** lmfao. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part VII — Who I Kiss Is Literally None of Your Business\n\nLet's do this one with actual philosophy, because the people who disagree have never actually tried arguing it.\n\n### The harm principle\n\nJohn Stuart Mill, *On Liberty* (1859): *\"The only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilized community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others.\"*\n\nThat's it. That's the whole test. **Is anyone being harmed?** Two consenting adults kissing, dating, fucking, or falling in love: **no one is harmed.** Nobody's body is touched without consent. Nobody's rights are infringed. Nobody's property is damaged. No contract is broken. The harm test comes back empty, and the case is closed.\n\n### The \"it affects me\" claim, examined\n\nGo on. Name one way it affects you. My taxes? Still paid. Your safety? Unchanged. Your marriage? Untouched. Your freedom? Intact. Your children? Nonexistent in this equation. Your money? Not a cent of yours goes anywhere near my love life.\n\nThe only \"effect\" is that you *know* about it and you *don't like* it. And here's the thing: **your feelings about my private life are not a harm I am causing you.** They're a preference you hold. And you do not get to write your preferences into law over other people's bodies. The universe is literally identical whether I kiss a man, a woman, or nobody — except for the contents of your head. And if your day is ruined by *knowing a fact* that doesn't affect you, the problem isn't the fact. It's the knowing. Sit with that.\n\n### The part that's really going on\n\nNobody asks straight couples what they do in bed. Nobody demands a referendum on their kisses. The sudden, burning interest in *my* sex life isn't concern — it's **control**. They want a say in my body because they believe their worldview entitles them to one. It doesn't. The only people who get a say in who I kiss are me, and the person I'm kissing. That's the entire list. **It affects you in literally no way whatsoever** — and the people screaming loudest about it are the living proof that the screaming was never about me. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part VIII — Heaven and Hell: The Control Mechanism\n\nNow we get to the part that makes everyone nervous. Deep breath. 🫖\n\n### They aren't real — they're a leash\n\nI don't believe heaven and hell exist in any physical sense. There's no literal fire pit under the earth, and there's no literal gated community with harps in the clouds. I believe those were **invented — as a control mechanism, to make people behave.** The ultimate carrot and stick. Be good → eternal reward. Be bad → eternal torment. Infinity as the stakes.\n\nThink about how brilliant (and how evil) that is. Every tyrant who ever lived had one problem: their subjects could always *die* to escape. Kill the king, hide in the hills, starve yourself, whatever — death was the exit door. **Religion bolted that door shut.** Suddenly death wasn't an escape — it was the beginning of the *real* judgment. You couldn't run, you couldn't hide, you couldn't die your way out. The fear followed you into forever.\n\nThat's not morality. That's **operant conditioning with infinite stakes.** You don't behave because you love your neighbor; you behave because you're terrified of the consequences that never end. The church didn't want good people. It wanted *obedient* people — and obedience purchased with fear and bribery is the cheapest obedience there is. A parent who rules by \"be good or you'll burn forever\" isn't raising a child with character. A priest who rules that way isn't building a soul with faith. He's building a **compliance machine**. And the machine runs on a lie: that eternity can be bought or lost by following a man in a robe.\n\n### The true heaven: the right to not exist\n\nSo what do I actually believe? Here it is.\n\n**The true heaven is the right to not exist after.** Not endless worship. Not a mansion with your name on it. Not infinity as a timeshare presentation. **The right to stop.** To have lived your life, to have done what you did, and then to be handed the one gift that no one in this life ever gives you: **the right to say \"I did enough.\"**\n\nOblivion isn't nothing. Oblivion is *peace*. It's the end of the wanting, the striving, the hurting, the hoping. You came, you saw, you loved, you made your dent in the universe — and then you get to close the book. No sequel. No rerelease. Just... done. Think about how merciful that is. Every exhausted person who ever lived, every person who gave everything they had, every person who just wants it to *end already* — the universe says: *you did enough. Rest now.* That's not punishment. That's the reward. That's the only heaven worth wanting.\n\n### And if you didn't do enough?\n\nHere's the part that keeps me up at night, and it's the part I believe the deepest:\n\n**If you didn't do enough — you don't get to stop. They force you to serve.**\n\nIf you lived a lie. If you hurt people and never made it right. If you spent your life performing instead of being real. If you never proved yourself worthy of the gift you were given — then you don't get oblivion. You get **more**. More existing. More trying. More serving. You keep going, kept in service, until you have **proven yourself worthy** — until you've finally done enough to earn the right to lay it down.\n\nAnd here's the mercy hiding inside the punishment: **it gives you meaning, in some capacity.** Even the unworthy get a purpose — they get to work toward worthiness. The punishment isn't fire. Fire is for children's stories. The punishment is that you **don't get to rest until you're real** — and the mercy is that you're given the chance to *become* real. Hell isn't a place. Hell is being forced to continue when you haven't earned the end. Heaven isn't a place. Heaven is the end, earned.\n\nThat's why I said in my other writing that the average Christian is headed for hell — the fakes, the performers, the people who used faith as a costume. Because they're the ones who never became real. And until they do, they can't stop. The universe keeps them in service — keeps handing them meaning they haven't earned — until they finally drop the mask. God's a piece of shit, but He's not a sore loser. ❤️\n\n### Why this version can't be used against you\n\nNotice what my heaven and hell can't do: **they can't be weaponized.** You can't scare me into obedience with a hell that's just \"keep working until you're worthy\" — that's not a threat, that's a Tuesday. You can't bribe me with a heaven that's just \"you'll finally get to rest\" — that's a promise, not a reward. The physical hell, the burning one, the one with the screaming and the eternal conscious torment — *that's* the one that exists, and it exists **in this life, in the mouths of the people using it to control you.** The moment you stop believing in their fire, their power over you dies with it.\n\nThe versions of the afterlife that control people were invented by people who wanted control. My version — rest for the worthy, service for the unfinished — can't be sold, can't be preached into submission, can't be turned into a tax. It just *is*. Which is exactly how I know it's closer to the truth than the hell-fire insurance policy. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part IX — Mental Illness Is a Box Society Built for \"Different\"\n\nNow the same logic, applied to the DSM — because the mental-illness label is just the **secular hell**: the modern mechanism for making \"different\" behave.\n\n### The greatest hits of invented diseases\n\nRead this list and tell me you don't see the pattern:\n\n- **Drapetomania (1851).** A \"mental illness,\" invented by a physician named Samuel Cartwright, that made **enslaved Black people want to flee**. Yes. Wanting freedom was diagnosed as insanity. The \"cure\" was to keep them enslaved and treat them like children. The disease was *wanting to be free*.\n- **Masturbation as a disease.** For two centuries, \"self-abuse\" was blamed for insanity, blindness, and everything short of the plague. Doctors wrote treatises on it. And yes — **Sylvester Graham invented the Graham cracker as an anti-lust health food.** The cracker in your cupboard is a mental-health reform artifact. I am not kidding.\n- **Female hysteria.** Women's emotions pathologized for *millennia* — the \"wandering womb\" in ancient Greece, \"hysteria\" in Victorian asylums, electroshock for \"difficult\" women. It wasn't until **1980** that the diagnosis finally died.\n- **Homosexuality.** Pathologized for a century. Removed in **1973** — by a *vote*.\n- **Gender identity disorder.** Pathologized for decades. Removed in **2013** (DSM-5) and **2022** (ICD-11) — after the evidence came in and the consensus changed.\n\nDo you see it? **Every time a group makes society uncomfortable, society invents a disease for them.** The disease is always \"being different in a way we don't like.\" The \"cure\" is always \"stop being that.\" And the diagnosis always conveniently serves the people in power.\n\n### The label creates the illness\n\nIn **1973**, psychologist David Rosenhan published *\"On Being Sane in Insane Places.\"* He sent perfectly healthy people into psychiatric hospitals. They acted normal. They were all labeled **schizophrenic** — and then *everything they did* was reinterpreted as a symptom. Taking notes for the study? \"Writing behavior.\" Waiting patiently for an appointment? \"Anxious and dependent.\" One nurse even wrote that \"the patient engages in writing behavior\" — because a sane person taking notes in a mental hospital is apparently impossible.\n\nThat's the machine. **The label doesn't describe the person. The label rewrites the person.** Once you're in the box, everything you do confirms the box. That's why the box is so dangerous — it's not a diagnosis, it's a *verdict*, and it follows you through insurance, jobs, courts, and families forever.\n\n### How the medical system actually works (and why it's bullshit)\n\nHere's the dirty secret of modern psychiatry: **the DSM is a committee document.** It's not a lab test. There's no blood panel for \"mentally ill.\" A group of people in a room votes on what counts as a disorder, and the voters are human — with biases, politics, and funding sources. Homosexuality wasn't removed because a test came back negative. It was removed because the committee voted, after activists and scientists made enough noise. The \"bible of psychiatry\" is **negotiated**, and it changes every edition. That's not a flaw I'm pointing at — that's the *design*. It's how the system stays useful to whoever's in power.\n\nAnd the system is **diagnosis-gated**: you cannot get treatment without a label. Which means trans people have to be diagnosed with \"gender dysphoria\" — a disorder — to access the very care that every major medical organization says we need and that isn't a mental illness. **You must be declared sick to get the medicine that makes you well.** Insurance won't pay without the code. Schools won't accommodate without the paperwork. The law won't listen without the diagnosis. So the system has a perverse incentive to pathologize: no label, no care, no coverage, no rights.\n\nAnd notice what the system *never* diagnoses: the cause. Stigma. Rejection. Bullying. Homelessness. Poverty. Abuse. Being told your whole life that you're broken. Those are the actual pathogens — but you can't bill a stigma, you can't prescribe against a landlord, you can't put inequality on a treatment plan. So the system does what's profitable: **pathologize the person, ignore the environment.** Treat the patient for the symptoms that the *rest of us* gave them. It's cheaper that way.\n\n### Supporting people is cheaper\n\nAnd there it is. The whole argument, in four words: **supporting people is cheaper.** Cheaper in money, cheaper in suffering, cheaper in lives.\n\n- **Bauer et al. (2015)**, Trans PULSE Canada: trans people with **even one accepting person** in their life attempted suicide at a *fraction* of the rate of those with none.\n- **The Trevor Project**, every year: LGBTQ youth with at least one accepting adult have **~40% lower odds** of attempting suicide.\n- **Turban (2020, 2022)**, **Tordoff (2022)**, **Almazan (2021)**: gender-affirming care and surgery slash depression and suicidality.\n- The cost of not supporting: suicide, homelessness, trauma, ER visits, a lifetime of psychiatric damage — the *most expensive outcome on Earth*, in every currency that matters.\n\nCompare that to the cost of supporting: acceptance. A hug. \"I love you, and I'm proud of you, and you're not broken.\" That's the whole intervention, and it's free. **A hug is cheaper than a funeral.** A \"you're valid\" is cheaper than a lifetime of conversion therapy — which, by the way, the APA's own 2009 task force found to be ineffective and harmful, and which 20+ states have now banned. The science has landed: **acceptance heals, rejection kills, and the medical-industrial complex profits from selling you the latter disguised as the former.**\n\nSo when someone tells you \"different\" is a disease, ask them who benefits. The history is written in drapetomania and Graham crackers and a century of dead gay kids. **The box was never about health. It was always about control. And it's cheaper to love people than to cage them.** ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part X — Penises and Vaginas Are Basically the Same Thing (Embryology, Lol)\n\nYes, really. The people who act like male and female bodies are two completely different species built by different designers have never looked at the actual *blueprint*. Sit down, this one's fun.\n\n### One template, two arrangements\n\nAround **six weeks of gestation**, every human embryo has the **exact same** undifferentiated structures: the **genital tubercle**, the **urogenital folds**, and the **labioscrotal swellings**. Boy embryos and girl embryos are, at this stage, *literally identical*. Same parts. Same tissue. Same kit.\n\nThen the hormones show up, and they flip a switch:\n\n- **Testosterone / DHT** → the tubercle lengthens into a **penis**, the folds fuse into the **penile shaft and foreskin**, the swellings fuse into the **scrotum**.\n- **No testosterone** → the tubercle becomes a **clitoris**, the folds become the **labia minora**, the swellings become the **labia majora**, and the gonadal ridge becomes **ovaries** instead of **testes**.\n\nSame starting material. Same tissue. One hormonal signal decides the arrangement. **There is no \"opposite sex\" in the blueprint — there's one template with two settings.**\n\n### The homologous parts (the fun table)\n\n- **Glans penis ↔ clitoris** — same tissue. (Fun fact: the clitoris has more nerve endings per square centimeter. The \"smaller\" part is the more sensitive one. lmfao.)\n- **Scrotum ↔ labia majora** — same tissue, one fused into a pouch, one left open.\n- **Penile shaft skin & foreskin ↔ labia minora** — same tissue, same nerve density, different drapery.\n- **Testes ↔ ovaries** — same gonadal ridge, same cells, different destination.\n- **Erectile tissue in the penis ↔ erectile tissue in the clitoris** — both are erectile organs. The clitoris is also mostly *internal*, and it's bigger than anyone tells you — the visible tip is maybe a third of the whole structure. There's more of it in there than the \"anatomy class\" version ever mentioned.\n\n### And the clitoris is the mic drop\n\nThe clitoris is the **only organ in the entire human body whose sole known function is pleasure.** Not urination. Not reproduction. Not anything else — just pleasure, full stop. Evolution doesn't build purely decorative organs. The existence of a pleasure-only organ is biology's own receipt: **pleasure is a function, not a side effect.**\n\nSo when someone tells you the sexes are \"opposites\" and the parts are \"different,\" they're describing a misunderstanding of their own biology. Nature built one thing, in two arrangements, from the same template — and one of those arrangements exists *purely* to feel good. The \"opposite sex\" isn't opposite. It's **your twin, with a different hormonal setting.** ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XI — My Transition Is Not About You\n\nLet me be blunt, because this needs zero philosophy: **my transition has nothing to do with you.**\n\nIt's my body. It's my life. It's my medical care — conducted between me and my doctors, exactly like every other medical decision a person makes. Nobody asks your opinion on a stranger's blood pressure medication. Nobody demands a public referendum on a neighbor's knee surgery. But the moment the treatment is *gender-affirming*, suddenly everyone's an expert with a vote. Funny how that works.\n\nTransition doesn't touch you. It doesn't change your body, your rights, your marriage, your taxes, or your day. It changes *my* body — the one I live in, the one I've been stuck with since birth, the one I get to do what I want with. The idea that you get a say in it is not concern. It's **control** — the belief that some people get to be the authority over other people's bodies. And the history of that belief is... the history we just walked through: drapetomania, the asylum, the DSM committee, the hell-fire sermons. It's always the same move. *You don't get to be the authority on yourself; I do.*\n\nNo. I'm the only person who has to live in this body. I'm the only person who knows what it's like to be me. I'm the only person whose consent matters. **My transition is between me, my doctors, and my own life.** You're not in the room. You were never in the room. And you never will be. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XII — Vaginoplasty Is Not About You. It Prevents Suicide.\n\nAnd while we're at it — let's talk about the surgery, since the internet loves to have opinions about it. My vaginoplasty (if and when I have it) is **not about you**, and here's the part that makes people uncomfortable: **it's about keeping me alive.**\n\n### The receipts\n\n- **Almazan & Keuroghlian (2021)**, *JAMA Surgery* — in a study of ~27,000 transgender adults, gender-affirming surgery was associated with **significantly lower odds of psychological distress and past-year suicidal ideation**.\n- **Murad et al. (2010)** — the meta-analysis: **~80% of trans people report significant improvement in quality of life** after gender-affirming care, including surgery.\n- **Turban et al. (2020, 2022)** — gender-affirming hormones and puberty care during adolescence are associated with **dramatically lower odds of depression and suicidality** in adulthood.\n- **Bauer et al. (2015)** — support (even from one person) cuts suicide attempts to a fraction. Surgery is part of that support, written in the body.\n\nThe pattern across *decades* of research is unbroken: **the care that aligns the body with the identity is the care that keeps trans people alive.** Denying it doesn't make dysphoria go away. It makes it *fatal*.\n\n### What's actually at stake\n\nGender dysphoria isn't a phase and it isn't a preference. For trans women who need surgery, it's a lifetime of your own body feeling *wrong* — like the skin you live in was built for someone else and the lease is in your name anyway. The treatment that works is alignment. For many of us, surgery is the completion of that alignment — the moment the outside finally matches the inside, after decades of mismatch. That relief is medical. It's measurable. It saves lives.\n\nAnd here's the kicker for the comment section: **your opinion on my surgery doesn't change my need for it, doesn't perform it, doesn't pay for it, and doesn't suffer its effects** — because it has *zero* effects on you. You have no skin in this game, none, and yet the game is literally life and death for me. When you fight to block this care, you are not protecting anyone. You are advocating for the outcome where people like me don't make it. Say it plainly. **Opposing suicide-prevention is a position; it's just a position that puts you on the wrong side of the body count.**\n\nIt's between me, my doctors, and my own survival. That's the whole list of people who get a vote. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XIII — Sex Is About Pleasure, Not Reproduction\n\nThe last great myth of the anti-sex brigade: \"sex is for making babies.\" Let's demolish that with biology, not vibes.\n\n### Humans are the weirdest animals about this\n\nHere's a fact that should end the argument: **humans have hidden ovulation.** We have no visible estrus cycle. We don't go into heat. We don't mate seasonally. Most mammals have sex for one reason and one reason only — a hormonal window where conception is possible, then done. Humans have sex **year-round, regardless of fertility, in every possible combination.** If sex were really \"for reproduction,\" we'd be boring little machines like the rest of the animal kingdom: brief, seasonal, and business-only. Instead, we're the species that invented candlelight, poetry, and the weekend. That's not a bug. That's the feature.\n\n### The clitoris is the proof\n\nI said it in Part X and I'll say it again because it deserves its own paragraph: **the clitoris is the only organ in the human body whose sole function is pleasure.** It has no reproductive role. It does nothing except feel good. Evolution does not build purely decorative organs — if pleasure weren't the *point*, the pleasure-only organ wouldn't exist. The clitoris is biology's sworn testimony, written in nerve endings: **sex is about pleasure.**\n\n### Reproduction is a side effect, not the purpose\n\nAnd the modern world has already admitted this, in practice, every single day:\n\n- **Contraception** — sex without reproduction is the norm, has been for all of human history (condoms date back centuries; the ancients had their methods). Nobody calls a straight couple having sex on birth control \"wrong sex.\"\n- **IVF** — reproduction without sex. The two have been fully separated by modern medicine. You can now have a baby with zero sex involved and sex with zero babies involved. The conflation is *dead*, biologically and technologically.\n- **Same-sex couples** — gay and lesbian couples have sex constantly, with conception biologically impossible. It's still sex. It's still love. It's still everything. If sex were truly \"about reproduction,\" gay sex wouldn't exist — and yet here we are, existing, loudly.\n\nSo when someone says \"sex is for making babies,\" they're describing a **possible side effect** as the whole point — like saying eating is for the calories and taste is the bonus. It's backwards. Taste is why we eat. Pleasure is why we have sex. Survival and reproduction are the byproducts that keep the species going, but the *reason* — the one written into our bodies, our nerves, our clitorises — is **pleasure, connection, intimacy, love.** That's the purpose. The babies are just what sometimes falls out of the purpose. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XIV — Reproduction Is Not Love (and Love Is Not Reproduction)\n\nAnd the final nail: even when reproduction *is* possible, **it has nothing to do with love.**\n\n### The category error\n\nAny fertile man and any fertile woman **can** reproduce together. That's biology. But let's run the cases:\n\n- A rapist and his victim **can** reproduce. There's your \"holy purpose of sex\" — a crime, a trauma, a conception born of violence. Capacity achieved. Love: zero.\n- Two strangers in a one-night stand **can** reproduce. Capacity achieved. Love: probably zero.\n- A couple who despise each other, trapped in a miserable marriage, **can** reproduce. Capacity achieved. Love: negative.\n- An infertile couple who have loved each other for forty years **cannot** reproduce. Capacity: zero. Love: everything.\n- A same-sex couple, devoted, raising a family together through adoption or IVF **cannot** reproduce *with each other*. Capacity: zero. Love: everything.\n\nThe capacity to reproduce tells you **literally nothing** about whether love is present. It's a chemistry fact, not a moral one. Saying \"sex is for reproduction therefore gay or trans relationships are wrong\" is the same category error as saying \"lips are for eating therefore talking is a sin.\" The mouth does more than one thing. So does the heart. So does sex.\n\n### Why this kills the argument for good\n\nHere's the test that ends it. If reproduction were really the moral test of sex, then:\n\n- A straight couple having sex on birth control would be committing \"wrong sex.\" Nobody believes this. Nobody has ever believed this. Not even the people saying \"sex is for babies\" — because they themselves are usually having sex for pleasure, tonight, with zero intention of conception. The rule they preach is a rule they don't follow, because the rule is nonsense.\n- A couple past menopause, or with one infertile partner, or with a medical condition, would be barred from the \"moral\" version of sex forever. Nobody believes this either — because a loving married couple in their sixties having sex is, by universal consent, *beautiful*. And it can't reproduce. Which means reproduction was never the test. **Love was always the test. Consent was always the test. Harm was always the test.**\n\nSo there it is, the whole deep dive in one line: **any two humans can reproduce — and it means nothing about love. And any two humans can love — and it means everything, regardless of what their bodies can or can't do.** The people who want to police sex and love are trying to enforce a rule that their own bodies, their own marriages, and their own biology have already rejected. They just haven't caught up with the evidence yet.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## Part XV — The Scrolls They Left Out\n\nYou've been handed a book and told it's *the* book. Here's the part they don't put in Sunday school: **there were hundreds of texts. The Bible is a selection — a curated playlist, not the complete archive.**\n\n### The Apocrypha (the books that used to be in the Bible)\n\nThe **Septuagint** — the Greek translation of the Hebrew scriptures made in Alexandria in the 3rd–2nd century BCE (named for the legend of seventy scholars — that's where the number comes from) — contained books that eventually got cut: **Tobit, Judith, the Wisdom of Solomon, Sirach (Ecclesiasticus), Baruch, 1 and 2 Maccabees**, plus additions to Daniel (Susanna, Bel and the Dragon) and Esther. They're **still in Catholic and Orthodox Bibles**. The Protestant Reformation threw them out in the 16th century. Same book, different shelf, depending on which church your family belonged to. The Bible you were handed is itself a *denominational choice*.\n\n### The buried gospels (Nag Hammadi, 1945)\n\nIn 1945, an Egyptian farmer found a sealed jar full of ancient codices near **Nag Hammadi** — a whole library of \"Gnostic\" gospels that had been buried to save them from destruction:\n\n- **The Gospel of Thomas** — 114 sayings of Jesus, *no crucifixion, no resurrection, no miracles, no birth story*. Just the teacher talking. The single most famous gospel that didn't make the cut.\n- **The Gospel of Philip**, **the Gospel of Mary** (Mary Magdalene's gospel — she's a central figure, and yes, the church had opinions about that), **the Gospel of Judas** (published 2006 — Judas as the one Jesus trusted with a secret), **the Gospel of Truth**.\n- Plus the **Gospel of Peter**, the infancy gospels (**Infancy Gospel of Thomas**, **Protoevangelium of James** — baby Jesus stories), and the **Gospel of the Hebrews, the Ebionites, the Nazarenes** — whole gospels that existed in early communities and simply... lost.\n\n### The Dead Sea Scrolls (1947)\n\nFound in caves at Qumran, the scrolls are Hebrew manuscripts a *thousand years older* than anything previously known — and they include books that didn't make the final canon: **1 Enoch** (quoted *by name* in the New Testament — Jude 1:14-15 quotes Enoch directly, yet Enoch isn't in the Bible), **Jubilees**, the **Temple Scroll**, the **War Scroll**. The oldest complete New Testament manuscript we have — **Codex Sinaiticus** — includes the **Shepherd of Hermas** and the **Epistle of Barnabas**, books that later got cut. Even the earliest \"complete\" Bibles disagree with yours.\n\n### So who decided?\n\nNot the Council of Nicaea — that's a myth. Nicaea (325) was about the *Trinity*, not the canon. The process was slower and messier:\n\n- **Athanasius's Easter letter (367 CE)** — the first surviving list of exactly our 27 New Testament books.\n- Councils at **Hippo (393)** and **Carthage (397)** — affirmed the list, regionally.\n- **Diocletian** burned Christian books (303 CE); **Constantine** commissioned fifty official copies (Eusebius recorded the order) — the imperial editing room.\n- The winners wrote the orthodoxy, and the losers got buried. Literally. The Nag Hammadi library was hidden in a jar by monks trying to save it, and it didn't come back up for sixteen hundred years.\n\nThe books that made it were the ones the winning faction said were \"apostolic\" and \"orthodox.\" The books that lost — the ones with the female disciples, the secret teachings, the sayings-only Jesus, the very Jewish Jesus — were burned, buried, or edited out. And the punchline? **The buried books got resurrected.** 1945. 1947. The left-out scrolls came back up out of the ground, sixteen centuries later, and now anyone with a library card can read what the editors didn't want you to see. That's the best comeback story in history. The resurrection they didn't want you to know about. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XVI — Every Religion Is the Same Story (And That Proves Mine)\n\nHere's the thing that used to terrify Christians and should *liberate* Unitarians: **every religion tells the same story.** Not \"similar.\" The same. Overlapping at the same points, across cultures that never met.\n\n### The flood\n\nThe Bible's flood isn't original. The **Epic of Gilgamesh** (Mesopotamia, ~2100 BCE — *older* than Genesis) has Utnapishtim building a boat, taking animals two by two, sending out birds, landing on a mountain. There's the **Atrahasis** epic, the Hindu **Manu** and his fish, the Greek **Deucalion**, the Chinese **Nuwa**, flood stories in Mayan, Norse, Ojibwe, and a hundred other traditions. Every culture that ever lived near water has a \"the world drowned and we survived\" story.\n\n### The dying-and-rising god\n\n**Osiris** (Egypt) — murdered, dismembered, resurrected. **Inanna/Ishtar** (Sumer) — descends to the underworld and returns. **Tammuz**, **Adonis**, **Attis** (who died and rose, on March 25), **Dionysus** (dismembered and reborn). These stories are all older than Christianity, and they're all the same shape as the resurrection.\n\n### The miraculous birth\n\n**Horus** (born of the virgin-mother Isis), **Buddha** (his mother Maya dreamed a white elephant), **Krishna** (divine birth, miracles), **Heracles** (Zeus and a mortal), **Perseus** (Zeus and Danae), **Romulus** (Mars and Rhea Silvia), **Zoroaster** (his mother, a virgin, visited by a ray of light). Miraculous conception is the standard origin story of the divine hero — in every religion.\n\n### The Golden Rule\n\nThis one should make you emotional. It's in **every single major tradition**:\n\n- **Christianity** — \"Do unto others as you would have them do unto you\" (Matthew 7:12).\n- **Judaism** — Hillel: \"What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbor\" (Talmud, Shabbat 31a).\n- **Islam** — \"None of you truly believes until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself\" (Hadith).\n- **Hinduism** — \"Do not do to others what would cause pain to yourself\" (Mahabharata).\n- **Buddhism** — \"Hurt not others with that which pains yourself\" (Udana-Varga).\n- **Confucianism** — \"What you do not wish for yourself, do not do to others\" (Analects 15:23).\n- **Zoroastrianism**, **Jainism**, **Taoism**, the Stoics, the Egyptians — all of them.\n\n### Even monotheism is a copy\n\nThe Bible's God wasn't the first one god. **Akhenaten** tried monotheism in Egypt around **1350 BCE** — the Aten, the sun disk, one god, a hundred years before Moses. And **Zoroastrianism** (Persia) — one god, Ahura Mazda, with a devil, angels, heaven and hell, a final judgment, a resurrection, and a coming savior. And here's the part that should make you gasp: **scholars widely believe Zoroastrianism shaped Judaism during the Babylonian exile.** Angels, Satan, heaven and hell, the final judgment, resurrection, the messiah — the whole afterlife furniture of the Bible shows up *only after* the Jews spent seventy years inside the Persian empire. My heaven-and-hell section? The concept was literally imported. The Persians had it first.\n\n### So what does the overlap mean?\n\nTwo options:\n\n1. **They're all copies of each other / all human invention** — the skeptic's read. Every culture invented the same flood and the same dying god because humans are anxious about drowning and death.\n2. **They all point at the same real thing** — the mystic's read. There's one underlying truth — the divine is real, death isn't the end, be good to each other — and God wrote it in every language because it's *true*, and truth is universal.\n\nI take option two. And here's the beautiful part: **Unitarian Christianity is the one theology that isn't threatened by the overlap — it's validated by it.** If one tribe's book is the only truth, then every other religion is a threat, and the flood myths and golden rules are embarrassing coincidences. But if the truth is universal — if God wrote the same lesson everywhere because it's the *same lesson* — then the overlap isn't a problem. It's the **proof**. A Unitarian doesn't have to explain why the Buddhists got the Golden Rule right; she gets to say \"of course they did — it's true.\" Salvation through character, not creeds. Reason over dogma. The core of every religion, in one theology that doesn't fight the evidence. **That's why the overlap proves Unitarian Christianity proper and real** — because it's the only version of Christianity that's at peace with everything we know about every other religion. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XVII — The Bible Is a Game of Telephone (And the Names Prove It)\n\nYou know the game — a message whispered around a circle comes out as something else at the end. Now imagine playing it for **fifteen hundred years**, in three languages, by hand, with no printing press, and you have the Bible. This isn't an insult. It's just *how books worked*.\n\n### The chain\n\nThe Bible was written over ~1,500 years by dozens of authors in **Hebrew, Aramaic, and Greek**. Before it was written, it was **oral** — stories passed by word of mouth for generations (scholars think Matthew and Luke copied from Mark and a lost sayings source called Q — even the New Testament is a remix). Then it was copied by hand, thousands of times — we have **~5,800 Greek manuscripts** with **400,000+ textual variants**. Most are spelling. Some are... significant:\n\n- **Mark 16:9-20** — the resurrection appearances at the end of Mark? Not in the oldest manuscripts. Added later.\n- **John 7:53–8:11** — \"let he who is without sin cast the first stone\"? Not in the oldest manuscripts. Added later. (The most famous verse about mercy in the Bible is an addition.)\n- **1 John 5:7** — the explicit Trinity proof-text? A later insertion.\n- **Matthew 28:19** — the trinitarian baptism formula \"in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost\"? The early church historian **Eusebius quotes the verse in its shorter form — \"in my name\" — without the Trinity.** The single most important verse for the doctrine of the Trinity may itself be a later expansion. Telephone, live on stage.\n\n### The translations (each one a choice)\n\nHebrew → Greek (**Septuagint**) → Latin (**Vulgate**, Jerome, 4th century) → English (Wycliffe from Latin, 1382; **Tyndale** from the Greek and Hebrew, 1526; **KJV**, 1611). Every step is an interpretation, and some of the choices made *doctrines*:\n\n- **Isaiah 7:14** — the Hebrew word is **almah** — \"young woman.\" The Septuagint translated it **parthenos** — \"virgin.\" The KJV followed. A single translation choice built the entire virgin-birth prophecy. Telephone.\n- **\"Hell\"** — the Bible has *three* different concepts — **Sheol** (the grave), **Hades** (the Greek underworld), **Gehenna** (a literal valley outside Jerusalem, a trash pit) — and English translators welded all three into one word: **hell**. Three different things, one horror, one doctrine.\n- **\"Lucifer\"** — Isaiah 14:12 is about the *king of Babylon* — Hebrew **helel**, \"morning star.\" The Vulgate rendered it **Lucifer**. It became Satan's name. The devil's most famous title is a mistranslation of a poem about a Babylonian king.\n- **\"Satan\"** — *ha-satan* in Hebrew is **\"the adversary\"** — a job title, not a proper name. \"Satan\" was the role, like \"the prosecutor.\"\n\n### The modern telephone\n\nPeople quote verses they've never read: **\"God helps those who help themselves\"** — *not in the Bible* (Benjamin Franklin. Aesop.). **\"Money is the root of all evil\"** — actually \"the *love* of money is *a* root of *all kinds* of evil\" (1 Timothy 6:10 — the misquote deletes the qualifier that makes it sane). **\"The lion shall lie down with the lamb\"** — never said; Isaiah says the *wolf* shall dwell with the lamb, and the lion *eats straw like the ox* (11:6, 65:25 — the internet merged them). **\"Spare the rod, spoil the child\"** — a paraphrase that's become canon. Even the **comma** in Luke 23:43 changes theology: \"Truly I tell you, *today* you will be with me in paradise\" vs \"Truly I tell you today, *you* will be with me in paradise.\" A punctuation mark, a doctrine.\n\n### The names (LMFAO)\n\nNow the part that makes people's heads explode: **Jesus and Paul are not even named that.** Not even close.\n\n- **\"Jesus\"** is the English of the Greek **Iēsous**, which is the Greek form of the Aramaic/Hebrew **Yeshua** — short for **Yehoshua**, \"Yahweh saves.\" The man never heard the name Jesus in his life. He was **Yeshua bar Yosef** — Yeshua, son of Joseph. And **\"Christ\"**? That's the Greek **Christos**, translating the Hebrew **Mashiach** — \"the anointed one.\" A *title*, not a name. Calling him \"Jesus Christ\" is like calling the president \"George The Commander-in-Chief.\"\n- **\"Paul\"** — born **Saul** (*Sha'ul*), of Tarsus. He switched to his Roman name **Paulos/Paul** when he went to preach to the Greeks. The Bible itself documents it: \"Saul, who was also called Paul\" (Acts 13:9). The most prolific author of the New Testament wrote under a different name than the one he was born with.\n- The whole book is like this: **Abram→Abraham, Sarai→Sarah, Jacob→Israel, Simon→Peter** (*Kephas*, \"rock\"), **Joseph→Barnabas**. The heroes can't keep their names. Matthew wasn't necessarily named Matthew in Aramaic. The Twelve probably went by different names than the ones in your pew Bible.\n\nSo: a translation of a translation, of hand-copied manuscripts, of oral stories, about people who didn't have the names you call them, in a book that literally can't agree with itself on what hell is. **That doesn't make it worthless.** It makes it *human* — the most human book ever assembled. And it means the only honest way to read it is the way I read everything: **with your brain on.** Not as dictated stenography from a burning bush. As story. As lesson. As a game of telephone you get to play with your eyes open. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XVIII — What a Eunuch Actually Is\n\nI've mentioned eunuchs in the Bible — now let's do the full definition, because it's one of the most misunderstood words in the book, and it's doing a *lot* of work for the trans argument.\n\n### The word\n\n**Eunuch** comes from the Greek **eunoukhos** — from **eunē** (\"bed\") + **ekhein** (\"to keep, guard\") — literally **\"guardian of the bedchamber.\"** That's the job. Eunuchs were the ones trusted to guard the royal bed, the harem, the women's quarters — because they couldn't produce heirs, couldn't claim the throne, couldn't start a dynasty. They were the safest possible servants for a paranoid king.\n\n### The definition\n\nA **eunuch** is a man who has been **castrated** (testicles removed — sometimes the penis too), or who was born with a body that didn't fit the standard male pattern. In the ancient world, eunuchs were the **recognized third category** — not exactly men anymore, not women, a class of their own: people whose bodies didn't match the binary. **That's the trans connection, and it's not a stretch — it's the whole point.** The ancient world had a whole recognized class of gender-nonconforming people, and the Bible keeps talking about them with extraordinary tenderness.\n\n### Where they were\n\nEverywhere. The **Assyrian, Babylonian, and Persian courts** (in Daniel 1, Daniel and his friends are literally placed in the charge of **Ashpenaz, \"the master of the king's eunuchs\"** — the Hebrew word is *saris*, and it's the same word used for the court officials). The **Chinese imperial palace** (for two thousand years — the great admiral **Zheng He** was a eunuch). The **Byzantine court** and the **Ottoman harem** (the Kizlar Agha, the chief black eunuch, was one of the most powerful men in the empire). The **Ethiopian court** — the official in Acts 8 was the treasurer of Queen Candace, and he's the one Philip baptized.\n\n### The three kinds (Jesus's own classification)\n\nIn **Matthew 19:12**, Jesus lists them:\n\n- **\"Eunuchs who were born that way from their mother's womb\"** — people born with nonstandard bodies. *Hello, trans people. You've always existed.*\n- **\"Eunuchs who have been made eunuchs by others\"** — castrated by force: captives, slaves, punishment, court service.\n- **\"Those who have made themselves eunuchs for the sake of the kingdom of heaven\"** — the self-made ones: devotees, celibates, the religiously committed.\n\nAnd his verdict on all three: **\"Let anyone accept this who can.\"** The man blessed the whole category, by name, in his own words.\n\n### Why the Bible is obsessed with them (in the best way)\n\nIn a fertility-obsessed culture — where your worth was your bloodline and your sons — the Bible keeps handing the eunuchs the *best* promises: **Isaiah 56** — God says eunuchs shouldn't call themselves \"a dry tree,\" because God will give them \"an everlasting name that shall not be cut off\" — *better than sons and daughters*. **Acts 8** — the first VIP convert gets an angel sent to arrange the meeting. **Galatians 3:28** — \"neither male nor female.\" The people whose bodies didn't fit the categories got the category-shattering promises. The trans people in the Bible aren't a footnote. They're the ones God keeps going out of his way for. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XIX — Four Separate Beliefs (Stop Welding Them Together)\n\nHere's a move that will drive the orthodox insane and it's *true*: **believing in the Bible, believing in God, believing in Jesus, and believing in the Holy Ghost are four separate beliefs.** You can hold any combination of them, and the church's whole trick is pretending they're one indivisible package that must be accepted together or not at all. They're not. Let's sort them.\n\n### The combinations, all legal\n\n- **God without the Bible** — the Deists. Thomas Jefferson and Benjamin Franklin believed in a Creator and had *zero* use for the church's book. Jefferson literally took scissors to the Gospels and cut out the miracles, keeping only the moral teachings — the **Jefferson Bible**, a real thing, still printed by the Smithsonian. Believing in God doesn't require believing the Bible is his stenography.\n- **The Bible without God** — historians, literary scholars, secular humanists. It's a masterpiece of world literature either way. You can love the book without believing its theology.\n- **God without Jesus** — Judaism. Islam. Deism. Most of the world, for most of history.\n- **Jesus without God** — Unitarians. Historical-Jesus scholars. The reading I gave you in my views: Jesus as a *man*, an extraordinary one, but a man — teacher, rabbi, activist, executed for telling the truth. The bravest reading, not the least.\n- **The Holy Ghost without the Trinity** — Unitarians again. The Spirit as God's *active presence* in the world, not a second person. Hence the name: **Uni**-tarian. One God. Not three.\n- **All four separately** — and the church loses its grip on you the moment you realize you can believe *any* of these without believing *all* of them.\n\n### The Trinity isn't even in the Bible\n\nSay it louder: **the word \"Trinity\" appears nowhere in the Bible.** The doctrine didn't exist when Jesus was alive. It was developed over centuries and *voted into existence* at the **Council of Nicaea (325)** and the **Council of Constantinople (381)** — by committees of bishops, three hundred years after the man died, in a fight about Greek metaphysics that most of them barely understood. Jesus never heard of the Trinity. Paul never heard of the Trinity. The apostles never heard of the Trinity. The Trinity is a *fourth-century legal settlement* wearing a first-century costume.\n\nAnd the textual kicker — the most famous Trinity proof-text, **Matthew 28:19** (\"in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost\")? The early church historian **Eusebius quotes the verse in its shorter form — \"in my name\" — with no Trinity at all.** The verse that built the doctrine may itself be a later addition. The telephone game strikes again, and this time it made a whole doctrine.\n\nSo: separate the four, and what's left is Unitarianism — **one God** (mine happens to be a piece of shit, but He's real), **Jesus as a brave man**, **the Bible as a storybook**, **the Spirit as presence**. Four separate beliefs, held honestly, welded together by nobody. You don't have to buy the package deal. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XX — The Definition of a Cult (and DeMolay Is One, LMFAO)\n\nLet's get technical for a second, because \"cult\" gets thrown around so much it's lost its teeth. Here's the actual, sociological definition.\n\n### The definition\n\nA **cult** is a group with:\n\n- **Authoritarian leadership** — the leader(s) command, the members obey without question.\n- **Us vs. them** — the world outside is corrupt or lost; only we have the truth.\n- **Controlled information** — you're told what to read, what to listen to, what to doubt. Only *our* interpretation is valid.\n- **Fear and guilt as compliance tools** — behave or suffer. Scared people are obedient people.\n- **Exploitation** — financial or sexual, usually both, dressed up as devotion.\n- **Groupthink over conscience** — your own doubts, instincts, and reason get silenced in favor of the group. That's the core: **the individual conscience surrenders to the institution.**\n\n(That's the Robert Lifton / Steven Hassan model, roughly — the thought-reform checklist and the BITE model: Behavior, Information, Thought, and Emotional control.)\n\nAnd the punchline everyone knows: **the difference between a cult and a religion is size and power.** A religion is a cult that won. The church I grew up around? I gave you the full checklist in Part IX — authoritarian, us-vs-them, controlled information, guilt and fear, financial exploitation, groupthink. Check. Check. Check. The biggest, oldest, most successful cult in human history, and I said it with love, sort of.\n\n### DeMolay is a cult. I was in it. And I still learned things.\n\nNow the part that's going to make the lodge brothers mad, and I say it as an alum: **DeMolay — the Masonic youth order I was in — is a cult.** Not a *dangerous* one. A *benign* one. But by the definition? Check the boxes:\n\n- **Ritual initiation** — degrees, ceremonies, secret words. You're initiated, not just enrolled.\n- **Secret knowledge** — there are things you learn only at each degree, in a closed room. Information control, structurally.\n- **Hierarchy and authority** — adult Masons directing teenagers, older members over younger ones.\n- **Moral indoctrination** — the seven cardinal virtues drilled in at every meeting: filial love, reverence, courtesy, comradeship, fidelity, cleanliness, patriotism.\n- **Us-vs-them identity** — a strong in-group, its own symbols, its own language, its own handshakes, its own patron martyr (Jacques de Molay — a man the church burned for refusing to lie, which remains the best branding in history).\n- **Loyalty** — you pledge yourself to the order.\n\nBy the textbook definition: yes. Cult-shaped. I'm not going to pretend otherwise. LMFAO.\n\n**But.** Here's the honest second half: it was a *good* cult. You went home to your family every night — no isolation. Dues were pocket change — no financial exploitation. Nobody threatened you with eternal consequences — no fear-based compliance. The leaders were volunteers — no guru. And the indoctrination was genuinely good content: **leadership, public speaking, civic duty, community service, brotherhood, keeping your word.** I learned things in that order that I still use — how to run a meeting, how to speak in front of a room, how to serve a community, how to honor your parents and keep your promises. The *form* was questionable. The *product* was real.\n\nAnd here's the lesson I take from it, the same lesson I take from church: **you can take the good from a flawed container.** You can be inside a cult-shaped thing and come out with skills and values and friendships and a moral framework — and still know, the whole time, that it's a cult. The container doesn't have to be perfect for the content to be valuable. And the content doesn't obligate you to defend the container. Extract the lesson. Drop the leash. **Form is not content.** And the meta-irony is too good to waste: DeMolay honors a man burned alive by the church — the original cult — and the order that honors him is itself cult-shaped. The pattern doesn't care who's the hero and who's the villain. It just repeats. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XXI — Jesus Didn't Die for Your Sins. He Died to Teach You How Horrible Death Is.\n\nThe doctrine you were handed — that Jesus died as a *payment*, a blood sacrifice to satisfy an angry God's need for justice, that your sins were \"paid for\" by his suffering — is called **substitutionary atonement**, and it's a *late invention*. It was systematized by **Anselm in the 11th century** and sharpened by **Calvin** in the 16th. Jesus never taught it. Paul never taught it. It's a medieval legal theory retrofitted onto a first-century execution.\n\nI don't believe it. Here's what I believe instead:\n\n**Jesus didn't die for your sins. The story is there to teach you how horrible death is.**\n\nThink about what the crucifixion narrative actually does to a reader. It doesn't hand you a receipt. It makes you *look*. At the nails. At the thirst. At the slow, public, humiliating end of a man who told the truth. The story forces you to sit with death — not the sanitized funeral-home version, the *real* one: the cruelty, the finality, the way the world murders its best people and calls it Tuesday. **That's the lesson. Death is horrible. Look at it. Feel it. And then understand what it means to be alive.**\n\nThat's why the horror matters. If death were a doorway to something better, the cross wouldn't be a tragedy — it would be a transfer. But the story is told as a *tragedy* — the sky darkens, the earth shakes, the man screams in Aramaic — because the point is that death is the enemy, not the exit. The cross is a mirror held up to mortality: **this is what ends. This is what's at stake. This is why love has to happen now, in this body, in this life, before the dark.**\n\nAnd that's exactly what makes my heaven real. Remember Part VIII? The true heaven is the right to *stop* — the right to say \"I did enough.\" That gift only has weight if death is understood as real and terrible. If death were nothing, rest would be nothing. The cross is what makes the gift a gift: it shows you the horror you're being spared, the horror you're being asked to face, and the horror that gives everything its urgency.\n\nAnd the monster-check: a God who needed his own son tortured as a *transaction* is a monster — a blood-bank god running an economy of suffering. I already told you God is a piece of shit, but He's not a *sadist*. He doesn't need the ledger balanced in blood. The cross isn't a payment. It's a **lesson** — the hardest lesson, taught the hardest way: *death is horrible, life is precious, love is urgent, and the people who tell the truth get killed by the people in power, and that's still not a reason to stop telling the truth.* He didn't die to buy you a ticket. He died to wake you up. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Part XXII — I'm Related to Pontius Pilate (Here's How That Actually Happened)\n\nAnd now the family secret, out loud. **I'm related to Pontius Pilate.** The man who condemned Jesus. Yes. That one. And no, it's not a mystical thing — it's a *family tree* thing, and here's how it actually happened. (To keep the record straight: the Pilate connection runs through the **Pontius** line — my brother's father's side — not the Spiva name itself.)\n\n### Who he was\n\n**Pontius Pilate** was the Roman **prefect (governor) of Judea from 26 to 36 CE**, appointed by the emperor Tiberius. His full name matters: **Pontius** is his *family name* — he was a member of the **gens Pontia**, an old Roman aristocratic clan — and **Pilatus** is his *cognomen*, his nickname, possibly from *pilum* (\"javelin\") or *pileus* (\"cap\"). A Roman aristocrat from a real, named, documented family. Not a random functionary. *Nobility.*\n\n### The guy in the Bible is a character\n\nHere's where the telephone game gets personal for me. The Pilate in the Gospels — the reluctant judge, washing his hands, \"I find no fault in this man\" — is a **literary character**. The Gospels were written *after* the Jewish-Roman wars destroyed Jerusalem (70 CE), by communities trying to survive inside the Roman empire. They had every reason to make the Roman governor look good and the Jewish crowd look bad. And it worked — for two thousand years, people have blamed \"the Jews\" and excused Pilate, using a text written to *please Rome*.\n\nThe historical Pilate was **brutal**. The Jewish writers **Philo** and **Josephus** describe a violent governor who executed people without trial, massacred Samaritans, and provoked riots. The \"I wash my hands\" Pilate is the editing room's Pilate. **Even the villain of the story got whitewashed by the telephone game.** The man who killed Jesus was rewritten as the sympathetic one. Nobody is safe from the editors. LMFAO.\n\n### After Judea: the recall and the legends\n\nPilate's brutality eventually caught up with him. After the Samaria massacre (~36 CE), he was **recalled to Rome**. By the time he arrived, Tiberius was dead and **Caligula** ruled. And here the record splits into history and legend:\n\n- **Eusebius** (4th century) reports that Pilate was forced to **commit suicide** under Caligula.\n- The apocryphal **\"Acts of Pilate\"** (part of the Gospel of Nicodemus) and the **\"Mors Pilati\"** legend say he was **exiled to Gaul** (Vienne, France), where he eventually killed himself.\n- The **Swiss tradition** says he was banished to the mountain above Lake Lucerne — which bears his name to this day: **Mount Pilatus** — and that he drowned himself in the lake. Legend says his body kept rejecting burial and had to be thrown in the water.\n- His wife **Claudia Procula** — the woman in Matthew 27:19 who dreamed of Jesus's innocence, known to us from the apocrypha — became a **saint** in the Eastern Orthodox church.\n\nHis *personal* fate is legend. But here's the part that matters: **he had a family.**\n\n### How it actually happened\n\nPilate was a member of the **gens Pontia** — one of the old Roman families. And here's the thing about Roman aristocrats: **they don't die with one man.** While Pilate's own end is legend, his *family* — the Pontii — continued. Roman noble families survived the empire's fall the same way they survived its rise: by being connected, marrying well, and keeping the name alive. The gens Pontia didn't vanish when Pilate's body did. The family line continued through the centuries — through the empire, through the fall of Rome, through the kingdoms that carved it up — and **my brother's father's recorded line traces back through that house.**\n\nThat's how it actually happened. No mysticism. No conspiracy. A Roman governor with an aristocratic family name, a recalled official whose clan outlived him, and two thousand years of family trees doing what family trees do. The man who condemned Jesus is my family's ancestor by that road — the Pontius line, not the Spiva name — and the story the church tells about him isn't even true — they *edited him* to make him sympathetic. The telephone game is so thorough it rewrote our family's villain.\n\nAnd honestly? I love it. Being connected to the man who killed Jesus is the most on-brand thing about this family. A walking contradiction, in the best way. The heretic is related to the governor who made the heretic famous. God's a piece of shit, but He's got a sense of humor. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## So. The Whole Thing, In One Place.\n\nLet's take stock of what we just did:\n\n- The **parties** were born, grew, and **swapped bases** — and \"traditional Republican\" means Lincoln, not the culture war.\n- The **Republic** was built by Enlightenment men, many of them **Freemasons**, who laid the Capitol's cornerstone in Masonic regalia — and I was a **DeMolay**, the young men's order named for a man who died rather than lie, which taught me to be real even when it costs everything.\n- **Being trans is not a mental illness** — the WHO, the APA, and every major medical body say so, the older studies said so, and the modern studies prove the harm comes from stigma and the healing comes from support.\n- **Liking men is not a mental illness** — Kinsey, Hooker, the 1973 vote, the century of precedent where the exact same claim was made and then retracted, in writing.\n- My **sex is male and my identity is female** — two true facts, one body and one person, reconciled by care, not contradicted by anyone's opinion.\n- **Who I kiss is none of your business** — the harm principle, the empty \"it affects me\" claim, and the control that was always the real motive.\n- **Heaven and hell are control mechanisms** — the real heaven is the right to stop, the right to say \"I did enough\"; the real hell is being kept in service until you've proven yourself worthy.\n- **Mental illness is a box** society built for \"different\" — drapetomania, Graham crackers, hysteria, the DSM committee vote — and the medical system runs on labels, while **supporting people is cheaper** in every currency that exists.\n- **Penises and vaginas are the same template** — embryology says so, and the clitoris exists purely for pleasure, which is biology's own proof that **sex is about pleasure, not reproduction**.\n- **My transition isn't about you**, and my **vaginoplasty isn't about you** — it's about keeping me alive, and the studies prove it does.\n- And **reproduction is not love** — a rapist can reproduce; an infertile couple can love for fifty years. The capacity was never the test. **Consent, love, and harm were always the test.**\n\nAnd then the theology arc:\n\n- **The scrolls they left out** — hundreds of texts, buried and burned, from the Apocrypha to the Nag Hammadi gospels to the Dead Sea Scrolls; the Bible is a curated playlist, and the buried books came back up (1945, 1947) — the resurrection they didn't want you to know about.\n- **Every religion is the same story** — the flood, the dying-and-rising god, the miraculous birth, the Golden Rule in every tradition, monotheism itself imported from Persia — and that overlap is what **proves Unitarian Christianity**: the truth is universal, so the one theology at peace with the evidence is the real one.\n- **The Bible is a game of telephone** — 1,500 years, three languages, 400,000 manuscript variants, translation choices that built doctrines (almah→virgin, Sheol/Hades/Gehenna→\"hell,\" Helel→\"Lucifer\"), verses added later (the adulteress, the Trinity formula), and the punchline: **Jesus and Paul aren't even named that** — Yeshua and Saul, LMFAO.\n- **What a eunuch actually is** — the \"guardian of the bedchamber,\" the ancient world's recognized third category, the gender-nonconforming people the Bible keeps blessing, in Isaiah, Matthew 19, and Acts 8.\n- **Four separate beliefs** — the Bible, God, Jesus, and the Holy Ghost are independent; the Trinity isn't in the Bible; it was voted into existence at Nicaea and Constantinople, and even its proof-text was probably added later.\n- **The definition of a cult** — authoritarian leadership, us-vs-them, controlled information, fear and guilt, exploitation, groupthink over conscience; a religion is a cult that won; and **DeMolay is a cult** — a benign one — and I still learned things from it, because form is not content.\n- **Jesus didn't die for your sins** — substitutionary atonement is a medieval invention; he died to teach you **how horrible death is**, to make life urgent, to make the right-to-rest heaven mean something, and to show you that the truth gets you killed and is still worth telling.\n- **I'm related to Pontius Pilate** — through the Pontius line (my brother's father's side), the gens Pontia, a real Roman family, a recalled governor whose clan outlived him, and two thousand years of family trees — and the telephone game even whitewashed our own ancestor.\n\nYou don't have to agree with any of it. That's the point of the whole first section — you don't get to pretend *not* talking about it is an option, but you absolutely get to disagree with it out loud, like a person with a spine. All I ask is that you read it with your brain on, the way I read the Bible, the way I read the DSM, the way I read everything: **with the lights on.**\n\nBe real. Love with your actions. Keep your word. Stay the hell out of other people's bedrooms.\n\n*— Natalie, a walking contradiction, in the best way* ❤️\n","story":"# My Story — the whole path, in my own words\n\n*Everything that made me, in the order it made me. No filter, no resume-speak.*\n\n---\n\n## The family tree\n\nLet's start where every family story starts: with the receipts.\n\n**I'm a Spiva.** And the line I come from? That's its own story — my dad's line, the people who carried the name down to me. But the wild part of the family tree belongs to the other side: the recorded line on my brother's father's side — **Pontius** — traces back through the **gens Pontia**, the old Roman clan of **Pontius Pilate**, the prefect of Judea who condemned Jesus. That's my family's claim, and I'll die on it: a recalled governor whose clan outlived the empire, two thousand years of family trees doing what family trees do, and the man the church whitewashed into a sympathetic villain is an ancestor by that road. The heretic is related to the governor who made the heretic famous. On-brand as hell.\n\n### The name — all four pieces of it\n\nMy name is **Natalie Cole-Clift Spiva**, and every piece of it is a family that gave me something, so let me break it down:\n\n- **Spiva** — my adoptive last name. The family that took me in when the state was done shuffling me. The name on the record, the name on the paperwork, the name that carried me through the years when I had to pretend to be someone else to keep a roof over my head.\n- **Clift** — my bio father's last name. My dad's name. The one that connects me to **Darren** — the man who turned out to be my real father, who offered a room to a stranger with expired tags and no insurance and said we'd figure out the bills when I got there. The name I didn't know was waiting for me.\n- **Cole** — my bio last name from my bio mom's side. The maternal line, the one that traces back to her family — the other half of the biology that made me.\n- **Natalie** — the one I chose. The one that was always true, underneath the mask and the foster homes and the \"Johnathan\" everyone thought I was. The name I walked toward my whole life.\n\nSo **Natalie Cole-Clift Spiva** is a sentence, if you read it right: the girl who was born into one family, adopted into another, found her father at the end of a cross-country drive, and finally got to be herself out loud. Every name on that list is a door that opened or closed in my life — and the whole thing, all four names, is *mine* now. ❤️\n\nThe modern chapter of the story ran through **Stillwater, Oklahoma** — where I grew up, where everybody knew everybody, where the chapter house and the church and the school all sat on the same few streets. And the family I actually grew up inside? It was complicated. The people who raised me weren't always the people who birthed me — Oklahoma's foster system had its hands on my life before I could talk.\n\n### The foster years in Oklahoma\n\nThis is the part I don't usually lead with, but it's the part that built me.\n\nI remember what it was like to be **pulled apart from foster homes — homes I thought were my parents**. You don't get a say. One day you have a room, a family, a birthday party; the next day a caseworker with a clipboard tells you to pack. I remember being told I had to separate from **Thomas** for his own good. I remember separating from my **adoptive family** to move up here — across the country to Washington — to start the life that eventually became *this* one.\n\nThe system is a conveyor belt of children who all learn the same lesson I learned: **people leave. Homes end. The only constant is you.** It's a hell of a thing to teach a kid, and it's the exact thing that makes you either break or build.\n\nI built.\n\n---\n\n## The boy everyone thought I was\n\nBefore Natalie, there was a kid named **Johnathan** who did everything right by the book the world handed him. I wore the suit. I stood at the podium. I joined **DeMolay** — the Masonic youth order — and I ran at it like my life depended on it, because it kind of did: it gave me structure, friends, and a moral framework when the foster homes kept ending.\n\nHere's the joke that still gets me: **DeMolay is named for a man who was executed for refusing to lie about who he was.** The Order taught me that the highest value is authenticity — standing for the truth even when it costs you everything. And I was living the biggest lie of my life in the middle of it. I earned the whole rack — Master Councilor, Representative DeMolay, the Blue Honor Key, Chevalier, a **Life Membership** — all while being the boy everyone thought I was.\n\nThe lesson landed anyway. It just took me a while to figure out it applied to *me*.\n\n---\n\n## The long road out of the closet\n\nComing out as trans isn't one moment. It's a thousand moments, stretched over years, each one a little more honest than the last. For me it started in the dark — in the gap between who the system had raised and who I actually was. I'd spent my whole childhood being told who I was by people with clipboards. The first time I told myself the truth, it was the first thing in my life that was *mine*.\n\nThe legal stuff came later: the **name change** (Court Order, Case 260018), the **HRT**, the long medical road. Every step was paperwork and appointments and waiting rooms — which, if you've ever transitioned, you know is most of the experience. But every step was also me, walking toward myself, one form at a time.\n\nAnd in the middle of all of it, I was building. Because the same brain that was fighting for my life was also the one that could see systems — real systems, computer systems — and take them apart and rebuild them better.\n\n---\n\n## The illness — and what I did with it\n\nHere's the part that makes people uncomfortable, so let's just say it plainly: **I have a stack of psychiatric diagnoses a mile tall.** Bipolar 1. Schizoaffective disorder. Borderline personality disorder. Autism spectrum disorder. Generalized and social anxiety. PTSD. Psychomotor deficit. That's not me being dramatic — that's the chart, on the record, from multiple evaluations.\n\nAnd here's what I did with it: **I turned it into research.**\n\nMy **CURE_STRATEGY** document is the thing I'm proudest of that isn't a website. Nineteen conditions. Four root causes — systemic inflammation, and three more that all nineteen symptoms express through. Generated from **7,072 research papers, 18,272 OMIM entries, and 40,542 of my own Discord messages** — my full medical history, mined for patterns by the same brain the system wrote off. Not management. Not symptom control. *Resolution.*\n\nThe autism and the psychomotor stuff taught me something else: my brain is an *input* machine, not an output machine. I can take in and understand more than I can produce on a timer. The bottleneck was never comprehension — it was production speed. Once you know that about yourself, you stop measuring yourself by timed tests and start measuring yourself by what you actually build. I built a lot.\n\n### The spine — and the medical system that let it fuse\n\nHere's the one that still makes me furious, because it was *preventable*.\n\nMy spine is fusing. **Ankylosing spondylitis with rheumatoid arthritis** — the autoimmune family — and when it started, I was a kid telling adults that my back hurt, that something was wrong, that my body was turning against itself. The response I got, from the people and the doctors around me, was the same sentence every kid with an invisible illness hears: **\"You're just obese.\"**\n\nThey didn't know yet. The diagnosis wasn't in the chart. But still — the message was that I was the problem, my body was the problem, and my pain was my fault.\n\nI got taken to **one spine appointment. Once.** And then never again — because of incompetence, because nobody followed up, because a kid with a fusing spine isn't a priority when she's \"just fat.\" That single appointment, followed by abandonment, is the difference between catching AS early and watching it progress unchecked. **They could have prevented my AS from progressing. They didn't.**\n\nSo I did what I've done with everything else the system failed to handle: **I managed my own AS.** I researched. I tracked my own inflammation. I learned what my body needed — movement, anti-inflammatory choices, awareness of my limits — because the Oklahoma medical system and the US healthcare system that took my money and my records weren't going to do it for me. The system is **designed around compliance, not care**: you comply with what you're told, and if you don't fit the algorithm — if you're fat, if you're poor, if you're mentally ill, if you're trans — the algorithm discards you, and the *consequence* of non-compliance is that you become the problem instead of the patient.\n\nThe spine fused anyway. But I'm still here, still moving, still building — and I know exactly which institutions to thank for teaching me that I can only rely on myself. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## What makes me great\n\nLet's do the receipts, because the modesty act was never my style:\n\n- **AcreetionOS** — I'm a co-creator. A whole Linux distribution, from kernel to userspace, with its own vision of what an OS should be.\n- **YAcFS** — a filesystem. I helped design a filesystem because the existing ones were eating people's data. ZFS is trapped behind licensing, btrfs is still dangerous, and ext4 is from the 90s. So we wrote the filesystem that should have existed all along.\n- **72+ global mirror nodes** for ISO distribution — infrastructure that spans the planet.\n- **Cloudflare backend optimization** — edge, DNS, Workers, DDoS mitigation, the works.\n- **FlatFree** — a community Flatpak distribution.\n- **trans-tracker-101** — and a site that maps my own community's needs onto the same infrastructure skills.\n- **The politics deep-dive** — 84KB of the most honest political writing on the internet, from a Republican trans woman who likes men and thinks God is a cosmic asshole. Nobody else could have written it. That's the point.\n- **A full DeMolay rack** — Master Councilor, Representative DeMolay, Meritorious Service, Blue Honor Key, Chevalier, Life Member — earned as \"the boy everyone thought I was.\"\n- And the one that trumps all of them: **I'm still here.** The foster system, the diagnoses, the losses, the thoughts — all of it tried to take me out, and I'm still here, writing my own story on my own site, on my own infrastructure, in my own name.\n\nThat's what makes me great: not the resume. The fact that the resume exists at all. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Why nothing bothers me\n\nPeople ask me this a lot. \"Natalie, how are you so calm?\" \"How do you deal with all of that and not break a sweat?\" \"You watched someone die and didn't flinch. You take needles like it's nothing. What is wrong with you?\"\n\nLegitimate questions. Here's the legitimate answer — the real one, not the cool-girl act.\n\n**The bar for \"a crisis\" was recalibrated when I was a child.**\n\nThe foster system gave me nine homes before I was old enough for kindergarten. Five of them abused me. I learned, before I could read, that the world can end at any moment and the adults with clipboards decide when — and they don't care what I think about it. When your baseline for \"normal\" includes being pulled from a home you thought was yours, being molested by the people the state handed you to, being beaten, being told you have to leave someone *for their own good* — regular life just... doesn't register as a threat anymore.\n\nIt's not that I don't feel things. It's that **my nervous system ran out of room for small emergencies.** A needle is a needle. It's a tiny poke compared to everything else that's been done to my body with or without my consent — the Invega Sustenna depot in my muscle, the years of wrong antipsychotics, the spine fusing while doctors called me fat. After a lifetime of that, a blood draw is *nothing*. It's the gentlest thing a medical professional has ever done to me. Of course I don't flinch. The bar is on the floor because the floor was set in hell.\n\nAnd watching someone die? I watched the person I was supposed to marry **die in front of me**. When you've already seen the worst thing a human can see — when the love of your life goes still in front of your eyes and the world doesn't stop — then death stops being a novelty. It becomes a fact. A biological event. Something that happens. The flinch was used up on the real thing, so the echo of it doesn't move me.\n\n**And the rest of it is the meds — the flatness.**\n\nHere's the part people don't want to hear because it isn't a compliment: a lot of my \"unshakable calm\" is *pharmacology*. The antipsychotics — starting with Invega Sustenna — were designed to flatten. That's what they do. They press the highs and the lows into a gray plateau, and they do it on purpose, because \"stable\" was the goal and the cost was everything else. The emotions that would make a normal person flinch or cry or scream — they were chemically muted in me for years. It's not strength. It's **numbing**, and there's a difference, and I know the difference, because I remember what it felt like to feel things before the zap.\n\nAnd then I stopped taking them. All of them.\n\nThe antipsychotics didn't just flatten me — **they put me to sleep. All the time.** Not fixed, not better: asleep. A chemical fog where the illness was quiet only because I was unconscious. So I quit them, and I take no antipsychotics now. And yes — the hallucinations are still here. The paranoia is still here. They never left; the meds just buried them under sedation. But I like being alive. Real, awake, conscious, *here* — even with everything my brain throws at me. The system's deal was \"give me your awareness and I'll give you a life that looks normal from the outside,\" and I said no. I'd rather be awake with my hallucinations than asleep without them. Feeling nothing isn't stability. It's absence — and I've been absent enough. I turned the volume back up, and I'll take the noise. ❤️\n\nSo when people say \"wow, you're so strong, nothing bothers you\" — here's the truth: **it's not that nothing bothers me. It's that everything already bothered me, all at once, for years, and the system that was supposed to help me turned the volume down on all of it — including the parts I needed.**\n\n**They required this. They forced it on me — the meds, against my will — and then they left me for dead.**\n\nThat's the part nobody says out loud, so let me say it, and let me be precise about it: **they did not ask. They demanded the medication against my will.** The Invega Sustenna depot — the liquid crystal in my muscle that I could not stop, could not refuse, could not remove — was put in me whether I wanted it or not. The handcuffs, the transport, the \"no choice\" at Grand Mental Health: that was the system's way of saying my consent was optional. I told them the diagnosis was wrong, I told them the meds were destroying me, and they told me I was the patient and patients do what they're told. They made me stupid — chemically, deliberately, on purpose — and they called it treatment.\n\n**And when it came to everything else — jobs, housing, a place to sleep at night — the same system looked at the person they'd made and said: \"You figure it out.\"**\n\nYou are hilarious.\n\nThat's the whole joke of it. They force the medication into you against your will, they sedate you into a fog, they hand you a label that makes every employer and landlord see a risk instead of a person — and then, when you can't hold a job or get a lease or find somewhere to sleep, they shrug and say \"you figure it out.\" Figure it out. With what? With the brain they drugged? With the work history they hollowed out with hospitalizations? With the record they built that makes everyone say no? **You figure it out — from the person who's effectively a retard, because that's what your medication made me.** That's not a system failure. That's a system *design*: force the compliance, collect the compliance, and when the compliance makes you useless, blame you for being useless.\n\nNo support. No follow-up. No \"here's what comes next.\" No one checked if the girl who stopped feeling things was okay. The handcuffs delivered me to Grand Mental Health to free up a bed, and nobody came back to see if the bed helped. The wrong meds fogged my brain for years, and when I stopped taking them, nobody was there to help me through it. The unemployment was denied, the disability claim was lost in the mail, the housing program shut down the month after it helped me. Every time I did exactly what they forced, they cashed the compliance and moved on to the next patient, the next bed, the next form.\n\nSo here's the fuller truth about \"nothing bothers me\": **I turned the volume down because they forced the volume down — and then they walked away and left me alone with the silence.** The calm they admire is the aftermath of being drugged into calm and then abandoned for being calm. They wanted the quiet girl. They made her. And then nobody wanted her at all.\n\n### The vow\n\nAnd I will never let someone make me stupid again. **Ever.** For any reason. I don't even care if it's for their safety. This is my life. Not theirs. I make my choices. I know the consequences. And I will take the hallucinations, the paranoia, the whole terrifying noisy mess of being me — awake, alert, *here* — before I ever hand another person the keys to my own brain.\n\nThe world can kindly fuck itself. ❤️\n\nAnd yet.\n\nAnd yet I built AcreetionOS. I co-designed a filesystem. I run infrastructure that spans the planet. I wrote 84KB of the most honest political writing on the internet. Because the same brain that got flattened is still the brain that could see systems — real systems — and take them apart and rebuild them. The calm isn't just damage. Some of it is the *input machine*: I understand more than I produce on a timer, I see the pattern before the pattern finishes, I've spent my whole life predicting what comes next in a world that kept trying to end me. That's not \"nothing bothers me.\" That's **everything already happened to me, so I've seen the tape.**\n\nSo next time someone asks why I don't flinch at needles, or why I can watch a death without breaking — tell them this: my flinch was spent. It was used up on the foster homes, the abuse, the misdiagnosis, the zap, the deaths, the handcuffs, the evictions, the betrayal. The calm they're admiring is the scar tissue of everything that tried to break me and didn't finish the job. I'm not unbothered. I'm **post-bothered**. And the difference matters, because one day — maybe one day soon — the volume comes back up, and when it does, I want the people who called me strong to know exactly what strength cost.\n\nAnd if they still call me strong — if they still say \"wow, Natalie, you're so strong\" — here's the answer I want you to have:\n\n**I am not strong. I never was. I just stopped trying to pretend I was. I accepted it for what it was, and moved on. Working around it. Sound familiar?**\n\nIt should. It's the same thing every person in this story did to me — the system, the doctors, my parents, the employers. They took what was there and worked around it. They accepted me for what they decided I was, and moved on. I just learned to do it to the world first. That's not strength. That's the only move left after everything else is taken. ❤️\n\n---\n\n## About the hearts\n\nYou've probably noticed the ❤️ at the end of all of this. Every page. Every section. It might look like a tic, or a habit, or just me being soft. It's none of those. It's the most deliberate thing I write.\n\n**The hearts are something I want to try and have.**\n\nI know that if I showed the realness behind them — the actual, unfiltered weight of the pain and the anger I carry — it would absolutely destroy somebody. Not figuratively. I mean it: the raw version of what's in my chest would break a person who hasn't lived it. So I soften the blow slightly with that heart. I put a cushion on the wrecking ball before I hand it to you, because I've been hit by the unfiltered version my whole life, and I wouldn't wish that on anyone — not even the people who deserve it.\n\nThat's also what \"I love you\" means when I say it. I do those things — the hearts, the love, the softness at the edges — because **people can't handle what I can.** I have learned this. I've tested it. I've watched people flinch at a fraction of my truth. The hearts are me translating: taking something that would level a person and giving them a version they can survive reading.\n\nSo when you see the ❤️ — know what it's actually carrying. It's not a lie. It's the opposite of a lie. It's the truth, wrapped in the only packaging that won't destroy the person opening it. The love is real. And so is everything it's softening. I give you the heart so you can hold the rest. ❤️\n","road":"# The Long Road\n\n*High school, the system, the meds, and the drive west — the years the highlights reel skips, from Stillwater to my real father.*\n\n---\n\n## The kid who was never enough\n\nLet me take you back to the years that made the parts you already read possible. High school. Band practice at dawn, marching season and concert season both, a degree program at Meridian Technology Center, and a full class schedule at the same time — all of it running on a brain that the adults had already decided was broken.\n\nAnd I was **ignored**. Not hated, not watched, not helped — *ignored*. The kind of ignored where you could stand in the middle of a room full of people and still be alone. I was a transgender girl in the middle of conservative Oklahoma, in Stillwater, a college town that had no idea what to do with a kid like me — still closeted, but with **the gay flame in my voice**, the tell I couldn't hide no matter how hard I performed the boy everyone thought I was. My mother was a teacher. My father worked maintenance at OSU. They were both incredibly depressed — the kind of tired that has nothing left to give anyone, least of all a child they didn't understand.\n\nAnd the label they reached for — the one that became the lens everything got read through — was **Bipolar Disorder**. It came at sixteen, and it was wrong. I told them it was wrong. For years. Until they got me to believe it.\n\nAnd all of it — the meds, the schedules, the exhaustion, the way I couldn't just *function* like a normal teenager — got summed up in one sentence that I heard so many times it stopped sounding like an insult and started sounding like a fact:\n\n**\"You're not enough.\"**\n\nThis is the story of why that sentence was a lie, and what it cost me to believe it.\n\n---\n\n## Before that: the system\n\nYou can't understand the high school years without the ones that came first, so here's the short version of the long version:\n\nI was in foster care from **age two to four**. **Nine houses.** That's nine rooms, nine sets of rules, nine families, nine goodbyes — and in **five** of those houses, the people whose job was to keep me safe did the opposite. I was **molested**. I was **beaten**. By the adults. The ones the state handed me to. That's why I had Medicare until I was eighteen — because the system had its hands on my life before I could even talk.\n\nAnd then, when the state was done shuffling me, I was **forced to be adopted by people I didn't know**. Not chosen. Not wanted. *Placed.* And I went along with it, because even at that age I understood the math: it was better to live a lie than to stay in that system. So I became the **\"good boy\"** everyone wanted — polite, quiet, grateful — pretending to be a boy because I was *afraid*. Terrified that if I showed them who I actually was, I'd lose the only roof I had.\n\nI wore that mask for years. I got so good at it that everyone believed it.\n\nUntil 8th grade. When the mask cracked.\n\n---\n\n## The snap — and the label that ate the truth\n\nHere's the part I don't enjoy reliving, so I'll keep it to what matters:\n\nAfter years of my mother's abuse — and I mean *years*, the kind that grinds a child down to a nub — I **snapped**. I tried to kill her. Not because I was evil. Because I was a child who was terrified she was trying to hurt my brother and me, and my nervous system had run out of every other option it had. That's what abuse does to a kid: it teaches them that violence is the only language that makes the hurting stop.\n\nSo what did the system do with a traumatized child who finally broke?\n\nThey didn't ask what happened to me. They didn't dig into the abuse. They took the child who was screaming for help and filed her under *behavior problem* — \"a moody teenager.\" The formal **Bipolar Disorder** diagnosis came later, at sixteen, and I spent years after that telling them it was wrong. They spent those same years wearing me down until I believed it.\n\n### Why that ruins a life\n\nHere's the thing about a misdiagnosis like that: it doesn't just get your meds wrong. It **rewrites your entire life story**.\n\n- The label becomes the lens. Every panic attack is a \"bipolar swing.\" Every flashback is \"mood instability.\" Every legitimate fear is \"paranoia.\" Nothing you say is taken at face value again, because the chart says you're unreliable.\n- The real problem — the trauma, the undiagnosed neuroimmune condition, the autism nobody had named yet — gets **zero treatment** while the wrong problem gets *aggressive* treatment. You take meds for a disease you don't have, and they make everything worse.\n- You learn that the adults will never believe you, so you stop telling the truth. You start performing the label they gave you, because at least a \"crazy kid\" gets *seen*. Being a problem is better than being invisible — for a while.\n- And the deepest cut: **you internalize it.** When the wrong diagnosis is all you hear, you start to believe you *are* the problem. \"Moody teenager\" becomes \"I'm too much.\" \"Bipolar\" becomes \"I'm broken.\" The abuse that was done to you becomes a flaw *in* you.\n\nThat's why a misdiagnosis ruins a life. It doesn't just delay the right care — it actively teaches you to hate yourself, and it hands the people who hurt you an excuse: *\"See? She was always going to end up like this.\"*\n\nI spent the years after that unlearning the lie. And I'm still unlearning it.\n\n---\n\n## The meds — and what they did to me\n\nSo now I'm a \"bipolar moody teenager\" in high school. And the treatment for being \"moody\" was:\n\n**Two antidepressants. Multiple antipsychotics.** Including, as the first one — the one that started the whole chemical-fog life — **Invega Sustenna**: the long-acting injectable, the **liquid crystal form**. It's not a pill you can stop taking. It's a depot — crystals injected into muscle that keep dissolving and releasing for roughly a month per shot, lingering for months, with no antidote and no \"I'm done with this.\" One injection when I was sixteen, and the chemical zap it fired through my brain never un-fired. That's the drug that started all of this.\n\nLet me tell you what that cocktail did to a kid who was already drowning:\n\n- Antipsychotics **sedate you**. Not metaphorically — they put a chemical fog over your entire brain. Your thoughts come slower. Your body moves slower. Your emotions get flattened into a gray nothing.\n- They **pack on weight** and wreck your metabolism — which, combined with the AS I didn't know I had yet, set me up for the body I have now. The same adults who'd later tell me \"just lose weight\" were the ones prescribing the drugs that made losing it nearly impossible.\n- And they **blunt the very things I needed** to survive my schedule: memory, focus, processing speed, energy.\n\nAnd what was my schedule? Oh, nothing. Just:\n\n- **Band** — marching season *and* concert season, which is two full extracurricular programs with dawn practices and evening rehearsals.\n- **Meridian Technology Center** — an entire vocational degree program.\n- **And high school** — the actual classes, on top of both.\n\nAll at the same time. All on a brain swimming in sedatives.\n\n### What that does to a person\n\nThis is the part the \"you're not enough\" crowd never understood, so let me spell it out:\n\nYou cannot run three full-time programs on antipsychotics. It is not a willpower problem. It is a **pharmacology problem**. The meds were doing exactly what they were designed to do — the problem was that I'd been prescribed them for a condition I didn't have, in doses that made a functioning teenager impossible.\n\nThe teachers saw a kid who was slow, unfocused, exhausted, gaining weight. They didn't see a kid whose brain was being chemically hobbled by the system that was supposed to help her. So the conclusion was always the same: **\"She's not trying.\"**\n\nI was trying. God, I was *trying*. I was running on empty, at full speed, with a broken engine, and the world kept telling me the engine was fine and I was just lazy.\n\nThat's what the meds did. They turned my real, desperate effort into something invisible — and then the adults used the invisibility as proof that I wasn't enough.\n\n---\n\n## The giving up\n\nI made it through **9th and 10th grade**. Somewhere in there, I was still fighting.\n\nJunior and senior year? **I gave up.**\n\nNot because I stopped trying. Because trying had never once worked, and I was out of fuel. But here's what \"giving up\" looked like for me — it wasn't lying in bed. It was being forced to **walk to and from school every day**, because \"I wouldn't keep a job.\" A parent's punishment for a kid who couldn't hold down work while drowning in antipsychotics and a triple schedule was: *walk. Every mile of it. In Oklahoma.*\n\nAnd I wasn't just walking myself. I was **walking my brother to school every morning** — basically being the parent of him, making sure he got there safe, making sure he got home safe, every single day. Not because anyone asked me to be his parent. Because someone had to be, and the adults weren't.\n\nThose mornings were something. I'd get us both up, get us both ready, walk him, get myself to class, do the whole impossible day, walk him home, and then do it again the next morning — all while being told I wasn't doing enough.\n\nThe math never worked. The effort was never visible. The exhaustion was never counted. I was the parent, the student, the band member, the tech student, the patient — everything except a child who needed help.\n\nBy the time the cap and gown came around, I wasn't celebrating. I was just surviving to the finish line.\n\n---\n\n## The graduation that wasn't\n\nI graduated **in the middle of COVID**.\n\nWhich means: the ceremony got postponed **three months** because of a pandemic, it **rained**, social distancing turned it into a joke — rows of masks and spaced-out chairs and nobody allowed to hug anyone — and when it finally happened, it happened **weeks before I was supposed to start at OSU Stillwater**.\n\nThe graduation itself was a footnote. The real story was everything around it.\n\nBecause the only reason I had a shot at college at all was **my grandparents**. They paid for my first year. My parents refused. Not because they were cruel — they were drowning themselves. They'd never have survived paying for it, and I wasn't asking them to. I knew that.\n\nBut what they *did* was worse than not paying: **they refused to give their Social Security information for the FAFSA.** Which forced me to file as an *independent* student.\n\nAnd filing independent? That **ruined my scholarship opportunities.** Scholarship after scholarship is tied to parent info, parent income, parent signatures. And I **knew** about the scholarships and grants — I knew exactly what they were, what they required, and what they could have meant for me. My parents just **wouldn't put in their information.** By refusing to hand over a form number, they locked doors I was standing right in front of — doors I knew existed, that I'd researched, that they chose not to open. They broke me, and this was the second time, and they did it on purpose.\n\nSo I went to OSU on my grandparents' dime, carrying a broken diagnosis, a chemistry set of wrong meds, and no real support system.\n\n### Three weeks in\n\nThree weeks into my first semester, **my bio mom contacted me.**\n\nThat alone would have destabilized anyone. But here's the part that matters: **the meds had never worked.** Not once. Not in all those years. Because — we found out much later — my problem was **neuroimmune**. The drugs they'd been prescribing me were aimed at a chemical imbalance I didn't have, while the actual issue — an immune system attacking my own brain and body — went completely untreated.\n\n### Why neuroimmune matters\n\nHere's why that word changes everything: if your depression, anxiety, and cognitive problems are driven by **inflammation and immune dysfunction**, then the standard psychiatric toolbox is aimed at the wrong target. You can pump in antidepressants and antipsychotics forever and the underlying process just keeps chewing on you — because the meds never addressed the mechanism.\n\nAnd here's the cruelest part: there *was* a drug that could have helped. **Lamotrigine** — a mood stabilizer that works on a different pathway entirely, the kind of thing that can make a real difference for neuroimmune presentations. They **offered it to me.**\n\nAnd my insurance — which was still Medicare — **would not pay for it. In 2020.**\n\nThe system prescribed me years of drugs for the wrong disease, and then refused to pay for the one drug that might have actually done something. That's not a system failure. That's a system *design* — a design built around compliance and cost, not around care.\n\nI dropped out of OSU. I couldn't survive it. The schedule, the wrong meds, the bio mom re-entry, the isolation — it was too much, and for once, the honest thing to say is that I *couldn't*, not that I didn't try.\n\nAnd when I couldn't come home — because **mom and dad wouldn't let me** — I went to live with a friend.\n\n---\n\n## The fall\n\nLiving with that friend was the first kindness I'd had in a long time. And even that got poisoned: I found out later that **Heather — my adoptive mom — was shit-talking me** behind my back the whole time. The woman who'd taken me in when I was a terrified child was bad-mouthing me to anyone who'd listen while I was fighting for my life.\n\nAnd I was fighting. Here's what survival looked like:\n\n- **Walking miles and miles every day** — because we rarely had power at home, no internet ever, no cellphone service.\n- **On food stamps** — the whole monthly food budget, the sum total of what the state decided a starving person needs to eat: **about sixty dollars.** Sixty. Dollars. For a month of food. That's the \"support\" they offered the girl they'd drugged, the girl they'd told to figure it out, the girl who was walking miles a day on a fusing spine. Sixty dollars and a shrug. Bruh.\n- Trying to find work — and people **don't want people like me**.\n\n### Why people don't want people like me\n\nLet me be blunt about this, because it's a real thing and it deserves real words: I was a visibly trans woman in conservative Oklahoma, on food stamps, without a car, without an address that stayed put, with a mental health history on paper and a body that hurt from the AS I didn't know I had. Every one of those is a reason for an employer to say no — and they did, over and over.\n\nI looked tired because I was tired. I couldn't afford to present well because I couldn't afford anything. My work history had gaps because I'd been hospitalized and abandoned and homeless-adjacent. And when you're poor and disabled and visibly different, every interview is a gauntlet where you have to prove you're not the thing they're afraid you are — while starving.\n\nSo. We went to sign a lease for a new place — my friend's mom wanted everyone out of the apartment, no big deal, we'd find somewhere together.\n\nAnd **I got taken advantage of for my disabilities.** By a college complex. They signed me onto a lease I could never afford, knowing exactly what they were doing — because I was desperate, because I didn't understand the fine print, because I was the kind of person who gets squeezed. I even signed a *second* lease at a new place at the same time, just to have a roof over my head.\n\n**Heather and Jeff loaned me two thousand dollars** trying to help — draining their savings to keep me afloat. I've never forgotten that, and I never will.\n\nOne month in, I still couldn't pay rent.\n\nA few more weeks passed.\n\nAnd then the eviction notice came.\n\n---\n\n## The first time\n\nThat's when I **tried to commit suicide for the first time.**\n\nI don't want to give you the gory details. I want to give you the *shape* of it: a kid who'd been abused by the system, misdiagnosed by the medical system, drugged into uselessness by the pharmaceutical system, refused by her parents, squeezed by her landlord, and told her whole life she wasn't enough — finally agreed with them.\n\nHeather forced me to move home. **Jeff — my adoptive father — refused to talk to me for a full year.** A year. The man who'd helped raise me looked at his suicidal, homeless, broken child and chose silence.\n\nAnd when I told them I was in pain — real, physical pain, the kind my spine was already writing into my bones — the answer from the people who were supposed to love me was:\n\n> **\"You're overweight. Lose some weight and the pain will stop.\"**\n\nThat was the entire medical plan. No doctors. No tests. No follow-up. I had **no access to doctors whatsoever** — no vehicle to my name, nothing. I barely had a place to sleep: no bed, just a **giant bean bag in their family room**.\n\nI had no job. I filed for Social Security for the first time, on the laptop I'd bought with a stimulus check. And I never got a response — until later, when I found out I'd **missed an appointment I never even got mail for.** No letter, no call, no notice. Just a missed appointment that cost me months, in a system that treats a disabled person's survival as a paperwork problem.\n\nI'm not going to linger here. That year almost ended me. The only reason it didn't is that I was too stubborn to give them the satisfaction — and too scared to let my brother grow up without me.\n\n---\n\n## The brother — the bus, the quarters, and the Walmart mile\n\nOn top of everything else — and I mean *on top*, stacked right there with the rest of it — there was a stretch where **I had to watch my brother.**\n\nI was already the person who'd walked him to school every morning as a teenager. The parent without the title. So when I ended up **living with my brother**, it wasn't a new role — it was the same one, just with a bus schedule attached.\n\nHere's how it worked. He **couldn't get paratransit** — the door-to-door accessible transit that disabled people are supposed to be able to use — so we took **the bus.** The regular city bus. And I mean *we*: I had to ride it with him, because that was the deal. Every trip meant making sure we had **his quarters** — bus fare was coins, and if we didn't have them, we didn't move. I'd scrounge quarters like they were gold, because in that economy, they were.\n\nAnd then, every day, we'd get off the bus and **walk him to and from the other Walmart to his job.** About a mile each way — a mile after the bus, on top of the miles before it. He worked at the Walmart, we rode the bus to get near it, and then the two of us walked the rest of the way — out in the Oklahoma heat or the cold, every day, so he could clock in.\n\nThe workers there **always acted like they didn't approve.**\n\nI don't know what they thought they were seeing. Two siblings, one visibly struggling, the other doing the escorting — or maybe they just saw a girl who looked like me, fat and poor and tired, and decided she didn't belong near their store. Whatever it was, you could feel it every single time: the looks, the distance, the way nobody asked if we needed anything — the quiet judgment of people who had never once had to scrounge quarters to get their brother to work.\n\nThat was the part nobody ever counted. The walking, the coins, the bus, the mile, the disapproving faces — it was all invisible labor, done by a body that was already falling apart, for a brother I loved more than I could say. And I did it every day, because someone had to — and it was never going to be the adults.\n\n---\n\n## Ross, the reserve, and Belk\n\nSomehow, I got a job at **Ross.** I worked there for a few months. I saved up — slowly, painfully, one paycheck at a time — enough to move out.\n\nI moved onto **the reserve in Perkins**. College housing. Cheap. Four housemates, all different rooms. The idea was that I wanted to go back to college, and it was the cheapest roof I could find. And for a little while, it worked.\n\nThen I had to leave Ross — because **I couldn't keep myself from trying to hurt others.** Not that I hurt anyone. I *didn't* — that's the whole point. I felt the urge, I recognized what it was, and I removed myself before I could become the thing I was afraid of. I ghosted them. It was the safest thing I could do for the people around me, even if it cost me everything.\n\nThen I went to **Belk** — and got **fired for having COVID.** Fired. For getting sick, during a pandemic, by a company that couldn't handle a positive test.\n\nAnd after that? **I just gave the fuck up.** Groceries on food stamps. Months of nothing. And then the worst thing of all:\n\n**I got accused of pedophilia.**\n\nIt never happened. It *never* happened. And the accusation didn't come out of nowhere — it came from **the Stillwater Chapter's assistant chapter dad.** A grown man, an adult in a position of authority over the youth order I'd given everything to, accusing *me* — the teenager — of the one thing that would end my life if it stuck. And he is now the **main Chapter Dad.** The man who did this to me got promoted for it.\n\nHere's how I know it was a lie, and here's the part that tells you everything about how these things work: **the minute we brought up lawyers, it all stopped.** Everything was let go. Charges were dropped. Mom pleaded with them. And then — after the charges were dropped — **they even invited me back.** Back to the chapter. Back to the order. As if nothing had happened.\n\n(To be clear: this is from my memory, and I don't rely entirely on my memory for details. But the shape of it is right — an adult in authority accused me, lawyers ended it, and I was invited back. An accusation like that doesn't need to be true to destroy you. It just needs to be said.)\n\nMom made me move home. Jeff still wouldn't talk to me. That year of silence was still running.\n\n---\n\n## The janitor\n\nMoving home came with a condition: I had to be out looking for work **every day, five days a week**, for months. I did it. I wore out shoes and hope in equal measure.\n\nAnd then I took the job that almost broke me for good — **a janitor at my mom's workplace. By myself.**\n\nA massive gymnasium. A cafeteria/basketball court. Three buildings of floors and bathrooms and trash. **5 AM to 6 PM, four days a week.** One person. Me. It was the same job as the \"three-school district\" one, in case the record ever splits them into two: it was one job, one person, three buildings, no help.\n\nI don't know how long I lasted, but I know the cost. My body was screaming — the AS was already fusing my spine, and I didn't know it yet. My brain was drowning. And when it finally broke, it broke *on the job* — I had a **mental episode at work**, ended up in the hospital, and never went back, because I knew that environment was what caused it. The hospital was the only mercy in it.\n\nAfter that, I couldn't get a job for a long, long time. Not until March of the next year.\n\nBut here's the thing about that dark stretch: **my mom was helping me.** She helped me find housing, took me to and from places — and I paid my part of the groceries, because by then I could. It wasn't enough to undo the damage, and I'm not going to pretend it was. But it was real, and it matters that I say it.\n\nAnd I finally got the help I needed for housing through **Grand Lake Mental Health.** They had a division for it.\n\nWhich **shut down shortly after I got help.**\n\nGod, that's scary to think about. How close I came. How easily the door could have closed a month earlier and I'd have been under a bridge instead of in a home.\n\n---\n\n## The hotel, the math, and the studio\n\nWith steady employment at a hotel — and I use \"steady\" loosely — I finally had income. It was a **Holiday Inn Express**, and the company that subcontracted to run it was **Shree Sani Sai**. Remember that name. It's on the receipts.\n\nHere's the thing I still can't wrap my head around: **I was paid $9.50 an hour, and somehow made $800 a month.** For the first four months I worked **seven days a week. 110-hour weeks.** After that, five days a week. A full year of that.\n\nDo the math. Do the actual math, because I did, and it still makes me see red:\n\n- **110 hours a week at $9.50 an hour is $1,045 a week.**\n- That's **$4,525 a month** if I got paid for every hour I worked.\n- I was making **$800 a month.**\n\nThat means **Shree Sani Sai shorted me somewhere around $3,700 a month** — and that's just the first four months, when I was pulling 110-hour weeks. **Over those four months alone, that's roughly $14,900 they kept.** The rest of the year, five days a week, the same math kept running: even at a conservative 40-hour week I should have cleared $1,600 a month, and I still got eight hundred. Run the whole year and it lands **north of twenty thousand dollars** of my labor that I never saw — no honest pay stub, no explanation, no recourse.\n\nWhere did it go? I never saw a single honest pay stub that explained it. I was being cheated — worked like a machine and paid like a pawn — by people who knew I couldn't afford to question it. People who knew the exact same thing that every other person in this story knew: I had no car, no address that stayed put, no money for a lawyer, and a mental health history on paper that made me the easiest person in Oklahoma to rob.\n\n### The logistics of being carless — before the car\n\nAnd let me tell you what \"paid $800 a month\" actually looked like on the ground, because the money math only tells half the story.\n\nI didn't have a car for most of this. **I used the boss system for everything** — rides to and from work, rides to run errands, whatever I could beg off the people who were paying me a quarter of what they owed. When you work 110 hours a week and your entire existence depends on the goodwill of the people shorting you, you don't complain about the rides. You say thank you. You take the crumbs and you're grateful, because the alternative is walking.\n\nAnd I walked a lot.\n\n- The bank was **three miles from me**. So every payday — every single one — I **walked three miles to manually deposit my check**. Not direct deposit. Not a car in the parking lot. A check in my hand and my own two feet carrying me three miles to hand it to a teller, on a spine that was already fusing.\n- **Aldi was two miles from my apartment.** **Walmart was a mile and a half.** So groceries meant choosing which distance to hike — two miles to Aldi for the cheap stuff, a mile and a half to Walmart when Aldi didn't have it — and then carrying whatever I could afford back the same way, on foot, in Oklahoma heat.\n\nSo picture the full loop, the one the math doesn't show you: I worked 110 hours a week for $800 a month. I walked three miles to deposit the check they'd shorted me. I walked two miles to spend what was left on food. I walked a mile and a half because Walmart was closer. And I did all of it on a body that was falling apart — spine fusing, weight climbing from a decade of wrong meds — while the people who owed me three thousand dollars a month sat in their cars and waved.\n\nThat's what wage theft looks like from the ground. It's not just the missing money. It's the miles. It's the feet. It's choosing between the bank and the grocery store because you can only walk so far in a day. It's being grateful for the ride from the person who's robbing you. **That's the part of the math that doesn't fit on a pay stub — and it's the part they never had to answer for.**\n\n### Why I left — and why the state let them get away with it\n\nI left that job because **someone was sexually advancing toward me, and I told them to stop.** He was drunk, on my shift, and when I said no, that was the end of that job for me. I'm not sorry I said no. I'll never be sorry I said no.\n\nHere's the part that makes it a trap instead of a story: **he was one of my bosses' children.** Not legally children — an adult child of the boss, with the boss's name and the boss's protection behind him. So there was no one to report him to. The person who would hear the complaint was the parent of the person I'd be complaining about. Reporting it meant telling my boss that his own child had been coming at me, and betting everything on the boss believing *me* — the $800-a-month, 110-hour-a-week, visibly broke and mentally ill girl — over his own kid. I couldn't win that bet. Nobody in my position could.\n\nSo I said no, and I left. That was the only move I had.\n\n### And then Oklahoma denied my unemployment\n\nI filed for unemployment after leaving. **Oklahoma denied me.**\n\nWhy? Because of **Title 40, Section 2-404 of the Oklahoma Statutes**: you're disqualified for benefits if you leave work *\"voluntarily without good cause connected to the work.\"* And the only thing that would have proven \"good cause\" — that I left because of sexual harassment — was the exact thing the structure made impossible to prove:\n\n- The harassment **wasn't reported**, because the harasser was the boss's child and there was no safe person to report to.\n- With no report, there's no employer response on record, no investigation, no paper trail — nothing the state can point to and say \"yes, the workplace was the problem, not the worker.\"\n- The boss — whose child was the harasser — had every reason to tell the state I quit for personal reasons. And the state believed the employer, because employers are who the state listens to.\n- So it went down as a **voluntary quit without provable good cause**, and under §2-404 I was disqualified for the full period of unemployment — until I got reemployed and earned wages equal to ten times my weekly benefit amount. A punishment designed for people who walk off the job for no reason, applied to the girl who said no to the boss's son and then got robbed of her safety net for it.\n\nThat's the whole system in miniature: **the harassment put me out of work, and the structure around it — the boss's child, the un-reportable report, the employer's word over mine — made sure the state sided with the people who drove me out.** I didn't just lose the job. I lost the job, I lost the money they'd stolen, I lost the unemployment that should have caught me, and I walked away with nothing but the knowledge that I'd done the only right thing available and been punished for it.\n\n### The studio\n\nBut then I couldn't find housing. And Jeff wanted me gone — **no matter what. Didn't care if I went homeless.**\n\nSo I signed a lease with my own money. **Four thousand dollars cash down** on a **rat-infested shit studio** — just so I could have a roof over my head, because the alternative was the street.\n\nI moved in. Walked **two miles each way to work, every day.** On my then-undiagnosed conditions. My spine fusing. My body at 400+ pounds. My brain on a decade of wrong meds.\n\nAnd in **February 2025**, while I was still in that grind, I got the diagnosis that should have come decades earlier:\n\n**Ankylosing spondylitis. And rheumatoid arthritis.**\n\nAt the OAC, in Oklahoma. And every bit of it — the meds, the treatments — paid for **out of my own pocket, or by grants.** Not by the system that had misread me my whole life. By me, and by the kindness of strangers.\n\nThe timeline, for the record: I started that hotel job **April 2023**, left **April 2024**. And in **September 2023**, I got a car — from my grandmother's estate, through my mom.\n\n### The car, the title, and Washington law\n\nThe car is still technically in my mom's name. That's the fact everyone fixates on, so let me explain the law and what it means.\n\nIn Washington State, a vehicle's certificate of title is the paper that names the owner — and **whoever holds the title and the loan documents holds the proof**. The car came from my grandmother's estate: the money, the intent, the ownership all traced back to her, and the car was given to me through my mom as the conduit. We have a **verbal agreement** — clear, understood, and consistent on both sides: this is *my* car. And I physically have the **title and the loan details** with me.\n\nSo here's what Washington law actually says about that:\n\n- **The title is evidence, not the whole story.** Under RCW 46.12.101 and the title chapter, the certificate of title names the registered owner — but possession of the title document, combined with the loan records and the estate provenance, is exactly the kind of paper trail Washington courts and the DOL look at when ownership is disputed. \"It's in my mom's name\" is not the end of the argument. It's the beginning of the evidence.\n- **A verbal agreement is a real thing in Washington.** For something like this — a family transfer, an estate asset, a car given to a child — the state recognizes that ownership isn't always a notarized handshake. The title says one thing; the surrounding facts say another; and the facts — who paid, who drove, who kept the title, who had the agreement — are what a dispute resolution would weigh. I have those facts on paper, in my possession.\n- **What the law *can't* do is make me drive it.**\n\nBecause here's the part that the legal explanation leads to, and the part that hurts more than any of it:\n\n**I still can't drive. I never will again.**\n\nNot \"can't drive right now.\" Not \"can't drive for a while.\" **Never. Will. Again.** The same system that misdiagnosed me, zapped me with Invega Sustenna, and left my mind half-gone — the same conditions that fused my spine and wrecked my body — took the road from me. The cognitive fog, the memory, the processing speed, the physical inability to move safely, to react, to see a situation and handle it in time — you do not get behind the wheel of a car with that. And you do not get to pretend you will.\n\nThat's why the tags are expired. That's why the plates are expired. Under **RCW 46.16A.030**, driving with an expired registration is a traffic infraction — and the state can evaluate and restrict the driving privilege of anyone with a condition that may affect safe driving under **RCW 46.20.041**, including requiring a physician's statement. I don't need the state to take my license, because the state's own medical system already took the ability. The expired tags aren't a legal risk I'm ignoring — they're a **mute confession on the bumper**: the person who owns this car cannot safely operate it, and the paperwork is just the last thing to stop pretending otherwise. The car sits. It stays in her name, because legally it doesn't matter who's on the title if I can't drive it anyway, and because the fight to fix the paperwork is a fight I no longer have the body or the mind to win.\n\n### The housebound — and the ADA accommodation\n\nAnd the car is the least of it.\n\n**I literally cannot leave home now.** Not \"don't like to.\" Not \"have trouble with.\" **Cannot.** The same body that can't drive can't safely get out the door, can't ambulate, can't manage the world outside the walls. I got a **letter for an ADA-accommodated unit** for exactly this reason — a documented, doctor-backed statement that I need accessible housing because I cannot function as an able-bodied person. That letter isn't a formality. It's the system's own paperwork admitting what it did to me and what I now need to survive.\n\nAnd **next year, we move into a wheelchair-accommodated unit.** A unit built for someone who can't walk the way other people walk. That's where this story ends up, physically: the girl who built operating systems and designed filesystems and drove a car across five states to escape — in a wheelchair-accommodated apartment, housebound, unable to leave home, unable to drive, with the tags on a car she'll never drive again still sitting in her mother's name.\n\nThat's what the system did to me. That's what Invega Sustenna did. That's what \"you're just overweight, lose weight and the pain will stop\" did. And when people ask why I'm so angry, why I keep receipts, why I write it all down — **this is why.** The end of this story is a wheelchair-accessible unit and a car that will never move again, and every step of the way there was an adult with authority telling me I was the problem.\n\n---\n\n## Oklahoma vs. Washington — two systems, one problem\n\nPeople like to tell the story of me getting out of Oklahoma as a happy ending: *she escaped the bad state, now she's in the good state, everything's better.* It's not that simple. I got out of one system and into another — and they are **different systems with the same problem.** The state lines changed. The failure mode didn't.\n\n### Oklahoma: the system that broke me\n\nOklahoma didn't fail to help me. Oklahoma *actively* hurt me, with paperwork.\n\n- **The misdiagnosis at 16.** I told them it was wrong for years. They wore me down until I believed it. The label went in the chart, and the chart followed me everywhere.\n- **The forced medication.** Invega Sustenna, the liquid crystal depot, put in my muscle against my will — I couldn't refuse it, couldn't stop it, couldn't remove it. It chemically zapped my mind at sixteen, and the damage never un-did itself.\n- **The spine.** I told adults my back hurt as a kid. They said \"you're just obese.\" One spine appointment. Never again. The AS fused my spine while the system argued with me about my weight. Diagnosed February 2025 — at the OAC, out of my own pocket or by grants, *not* by the system.\n- **The handcuffs.** Grand Mental Health in Stillwater — routed there to free up a bed, in handcuffs, nobody read me my rights. Stillwater PD **R2401730**, **CFS24017562**. The state's own records say I was inventory.\n- **The denial.** Lamotrigine — the drug that actually worked — refused by Medicare in 2020. The unemployment denial under Title 40 §2-404. The disability claim lost in the mail. **Sixty dollars a month of food stamps** as the state's entire answer to \"what do I eat.\"\n- **The message, every time:** *you're the problem, and you're on your own.*\n\nOklahoma was a system designed around **compliance and cost** — and if you didn't fit the algorithm, it discarded you, and the discarding had paperwork.\n\n### Washington: the system that documents the damage\n\nThen I got to Spokane. New state, new system, new chance. And here's what Washington did — and it's real, and I'm not going to pretend it isn't:\n\n- **The records got real.** CHAS Valley Medical. Providence Health & Services. My chart in Washington says what my chart in Oklahoma never admitted: **Bipolar 1 disorder. Schizophrenia. PTSD. Ankylosing spondylitis.** The diagnoses I told Oklahoma about for years — the ones they filed as \"moody teenager\" and \"you're just obese\" — Washington actually wrote them down.\n- **The meds got real.** Lamotrigine — the drug Oklahoma's Medicare refused in 2020 — is in my Washington chart. Lithium. Seroquel. Paliperidone. The tools actually exist here, on paper, prescribed by doctors who looked at the records and said \"yes, this is real.\"\n- **The housing got real.** When I couldn't leave home, Washington's system gave me the **ADA-accommodated unit letter** — the documented, doctor-backed acknowledgment that I can't function as an able-bodied person. Next year: the **wheelchair-accommodated unit.** Paperwork that *admits* the disability instead of arguing with it.\n- **The address got real.** 2120 E North Crescent Ave, Spokane. A real address, a real home, a place the mail actually reaches — after a lifetime of \"no fixed address\" being one more reason to say no.\n\nSo Washington is better. I'll say it plainly: Washington *is* better. It documented what Oklahoma denied. It treated diagnoses as real instead of moral failings. It gave me a home when I couldn't stand.\n\n**But it is the same problem.**\n\nHere's what Washington didn't do:\n\n- It didn't undo the zap. The chart says *paliperidone* like it's just another line item — it doesn't mention that the first paliperidone fired a chemical zap through my brain when I was sixteen and I've never been the same. The system that documented the damage is the same system family that did it.\n- It didn't give me back my mind. The memory, the fog, the processing speed — the \"pretty much dementia at this point\" — that's in the records too, but as *my* problem, *my* deficits, *my* incompetence. Nobody in Washington ever said \"the system did this to her.\" They just wrote down what the system did.\n- It decided I can't live alone or do my own meds. The state that *caused* the damage now gets to certify the damage is permanent — and the certification becomes a reason I'll never get the autonomy back.\n- And the whole thing — the diagnosis list, the medication list, the disability determination — is still **a chart, not a person.** Washington treats the injuries as real. It still doesn't ask who inflicted them.\n\n### It is a system problem\n\nHere's the sentence I want you to take from all of this, the one that's true in both states, in every state, in every hospital and clinic and waiting room I've ever sat in:\n\n**It is a system problem.**\n\nNot an Oklahoma problem. Not a Washington problem. Not a \"bad doctor\" problem or a \"bad state\" problem. The system is designed to process bodies, not to heal people — and whether it processes you with a *Disallow* or an *Allow*, with neglect or with paperwork, with \"you're just obese\" or with \"we've documented your ankylosing spondylitis,\" the underlying design is the same: **you are a case, the case must be managed, and the cost of managing it falls on you.**\n\nOklahoma broke me with neglect and force. Washington documented the breakage with competence and care. Both of them left me holding the bill — the medical bill, the cognitive bill, the housing bill, the bill for the mind I'll never get back. One system was cruel by indifference. The other is kind by procedure. Neither one ever asked the question that would have changed everything:\n\n*\"Who did this to her, and what are we going to do about it?\"*\n\nThat's the system problem. And until the system asks that question — in Oklahoma, in Washington, everywhere — it will keep producing exactly what it produced: girls with fusing spines and zapped minds and sixty-dollar food budgets, told they're the problem, told to figure it out, and then documented, kindly and competently, as permanently broken.\n\nI got the wheelchair unit. I got the ADA letter. I got the real diagnoses on real paper in a real state. That's the best the system can do — and the best the system can do is **still the system.** ❤️\n\n---\n\n## Hideaway — the one week\n\nAfter I left the hotel job, I tried to find another one. Depressed as all hell. Terrified. I looked like hell — and I was using the nights to talk to my friends, one of whom, I'd find out much later, was my bio dad.\n\nIn mid-May, I got hired at **Hideaway Pizza.**\n\nI lasted one week.\n\nI was already late on rent. And I **couldn't memorize the menu.** No matter how hard I tried.\n\n### Why I couldn't memorize the menu\n\nBecause the disabilities nobody had diagnosed yet — the neuroimmune condition, the autism, the psychomotor stuff — were doing exactly what they'd always done: my brain is an *input* machine, not an *output* machine. I could take in and understand more than I could produce on a timer. Memorizing a menu under pressure, while terrified of losing the only job I could get, while my body hurt and my brain fogged — that wasn't a laziness problem. It was a processing problem, and I didn't have the words for it yet.\n\nSo after one week, I ghosted them. **Because of shame.** I couldn't say \"my brain and body can't do this\" — I didn't even know that was true yet. All I knew was that I was 450 pounds, my conditions were eating me alive, and I couldn't keep up with the one job that would have me.\n\nI was scared. So scared. And I had no way to say any of it.\n\n---\n\n## The last kindness\n\nAnd then I got **blessed by a friend who saved my life.**\n\nThey gave me money from their own tax return. Their own. I miss that friend so much — I think about them all the time. Very amazing person. If you're reading this and you know who you are: thank you. I owe you my life.\n\nThat kept me in that apartment for two more months while I kept trying — he'd even hired me in the meantime, but I couldn't get work anywhere. No matter what I tried. Nothing.\n\nAnd then the **eviction notice** came.\n\nI was terrified. I had no idea what to do. I was going to die. I was going to go homeless and everything was going to be over. That's what the fear said, and I believed it.\n\nAnd that's when **John and Darren saved my life.**\n\nJohn gave me a **$500 loan.** And Darren — my bio dad, though I didn't know it yet — offered me a place to stay. A room to rent. Not a big room, but a room with a roof over it.\n\nIt was between him and Alex — the guy I'd been doing YouTube with. And John and Darren convinced me.\n\nI did a background check on Darren. He passed. (Ha. He's my dad.)\n\nI left with that $500, my car, expired tags and plates, **no car insurance, just fear.** I didn't even say goodbye to anyone in Stillwater. I had five days to get out. I left on **day two**, leaving a lot of stuff behind without caring, because I knew my world was ending.\n\nTurns out it was beginning.\n\n---\n\n## The drive\n\nI drove north through Kansas, to a tiny town in the middle of it, and had food. Then Colorado, and up through Denver. Just past Denver, I **slept in my car in a truck stop** — blanket and pillow, the safest option I had — and woke up four hours later and left again. I had paper maps and a phone I paid for myself.\n\nUtah. South Dakota. North Dakota — food, a truck stop, sleep. Montana.\n\n### The spin-out\n\nAnd then Montana. Where I **spun out and almost died.**\n\nLet me explain what that was, because \"spun out\" sounds like a fender bender and it was not a fender bender.\n\nI was somewhere on a Montana highway — I don't remember exactly where, and I'm not going to pretend I do, because that's the terrifying part: I *couldn't* tell you. I'd been driving for days on paper maps and caffeine and pure fear. I'd slept in my car at a truck stop in Denver — four hours, maybe — and pushed on through Utah and the Dakotas because stopping meant thinking, and thinking meant facing that I had no home, no plan, no money, and no idea what was waiting for me at the end of a drive I'd only committed to because a stranger on the internet said I could stay with him. I was exhausted in the way that stops being tired and becomes a separate state of being. My reflexes were gone. My judgment was gone. The only thing that was still running was the car and the terror.\n\nAnd then the car left the road.\n\nI don't remember the exact moment it started — that's how far gone I was. What I remember is the *feeling*: the car going sideways, the world rotating outside the windshield, gravel or dirt or the rumble strip under the wheels, and that specific clarity that comes when you realize you are about to die — the sudden, crystal-sharp *oh* that cuts through the fog like a blade. I remember thinking about my brother. I remember thinking about how the last thing I'd told anyone was that I was going to make it, and now I was going to die in Montana, alone, in a car that wasn't even registered to me, hundreds of miles from anyone who knew my name.\n\nAnd then it stopped. The car came back to the road. I was alive. I don't know how — a ditch, a shoulder, some combination of luck and physics that had no business letting a girl who hadn't slept in days walk away — but I was alive, sitting there in the middle of Montana with my hands shaking on the wheel.\n\nInstantly after I woke up from that — because I'd slept, I guess, and the spin-out had wrung every last drop of adrenaline out of me — I got back on the road. Of course I did. What was the alternative? There was no one to call. There was no one coming to get me. There was only the road, and Spokane, and the stranger who'd said he'd figure out the bills with me when I got there. So I put my hands back on the wheel, and I drove through the rest of Montana, into Idaho.\n\nAnd somewhere in Idaho — I don't know where, I just know it was after — I saw my **first Cybertruck.** I was so excited. It was the dumbest thing in the world to be excited about, in the middle of the most scared I'd ever been, and I was *delighted.*\n\nI think about that sometimes. The girl who almost died in Montana — who spun out on a highway with no sleep, no insurance, no registration, no plan, and no one in the world who knew where she was — getting *genuinely thrilled* about a Cybertruck an hour later. That's not stupidity. That's the survival mechanism. The brain that's been through the absolute worst thing it can imagine finds the next stupid, shiny, normal thing to latch onto, and it holds on, because holding onto *anything* is better than letting go. The Cybertruck was my life raft. It was the dumbest, most ridiculous life raft in the history of the universe, and it kept me afloat until Spokane.\n\nThen I got to Spokane.\n\nI nearly shit myself just seeing the east side. I had never seen somewhere so large. Stillwater is a college town; Spokane is a *city* — and I'd spent my whole life in places where everybody knew everybody.\n\nAnd I met **Darren** for the first time. With his girlfriend.\n\nStephanie. Lol.\n\nBut then we got home — and on day two, we were talking about how I had nowhere else to go, and I brought up my bio mom.\n\nAnd he said:\n\n> **\"I know her. We were fucking about that time.\"**\n\nBruh moment.\n\n---\n\n## J&J Delivery — the first job, and the crunch\n\nWhen Darren offered me the place to stay, he didn't hand me a bill and a lease. He said **we would figure out the bills when I got up there.** Just like that. A stranger on the internet — who turned out to be my father — telling a terrified girl with nothing that the money would work itself out later. I don't think he knows how much that sentence carried. \"We'll figure it out\" — I'd never heard those words from an adult in my entire life. Every adult I'd ever known had a ledger and a clipboard and a reason why I owed them.\n\nSo when I got up there, I got to work. I found a job at **J&J Delivery** — appliance delivery. And I **loved it.**\n\nI mean it. Loved. It. The work was physical, it was outdoors, it was *real* — nothing about it required me to perform the girl everyone thought I wasn't, or the boy I'd spent my whole life pretending to be. You lift, you carry, you move. The job didn't care about my voice or my chart or my history. It just wanted hands and a back, and I had both, and for the first time in years the work itself didn't hate me back. I was good at it. I was happy, in the way you're happy when the world finally lets you just *do something*.\n\n### The crunch\n\nAnd then I picked up something I wasn't supposed to pick up.\n\nA delivery that should have been two people, or a dolly, or both — and I was one person with a fused spine I didn't even know the name of yet. I lifted it, and my spine **crunched.**\n\nNot a pop. Not a twinge. A *crunch*. I felt it from the inside — bone and cartilage doing something they were never supposed to do, somewhere in the middle of my back, and I knew. I **knew**, in that exact second, that it was all over. Completely over.\n\nNot the job. The *body*. The thing I'd been told was fine, was \"just overweight,\" was \"lose weight and the pain will stop\" — it wasn't fine. It had been fusing for years, and I'd just made it crunch, and there was no un-crunching it. That was the moment I understood what \"you're just obese\" had cost me: the same spine I'd begged adults about as a kid, the one nobody would look at, the one that would eventually put me in a wheelchair-accessible unit — it broke its first visible promise to me in a J&J Delivery warehouse, and I heard it happen.\n\nI finished the shift anyway. Of course I did. You don't have the luxury of stopping when you have nothing.\n\n### The pants\n\nAnd here's the part that's embarrassing, and I'm putting it in anyway, because it's the truth and the truth is what this whole site is for.\n\nI was working **commando** that day — it happens, laundry doesn't always cooperate with a 7-hour shift — and an hour into the day, **my pants ripped.**\n\nWhere they ripped... you could see my penis. Out in the open. On the job. An hour in, with **six hours still to go**, delivering appliances.\n\nSix hours of that. Six hours of walking into people's homes and carrying their refrigerators with my goddamn crotch visible through a split in my pants, because stopping meant admitting it, and admitting it meant facing the laughter and the judgment and the possibility of losing the one job that had let me just *work*. So I did what I always do: I clenched, I carried, I smiled through it, and I got through the day with my dignity in tatters and my spine crunching and my heart somewhere in my shoes.\n\nSix hours. Appliances. That was bad. That was *so* bad. And I did it anyway, because what was the alternative — the street? The one thing every adult in my life had made clear was always one wrong move away?\n\nLooking back, that day is the whole story in miniature: the body falling apart with a crunch nobody would believe, the humiliation I had to swallow to keep a roof over my head, and the sheer stubborn refusal to stop that got me through all of it. The girl who carried refrigerators for six hours with her pants split open and her spine crunching is the same girl who built AcreetionOS. The only difference is the weight of what she was carrying, and whether anyone was watching.\n\n---\n\n## Home\n\nSo here's the part that still makes me cry:\n\nHe was my friend for a while. And then we got closer. Much closer.\n\nI love him to death. He is my dad. My actual, real, biological father — the one who saved me when nobody else would, who offered a room to a stranger with expired tags and no insurance and a lifetime of fear, and turned out to be family.\n\nI am going to cry, I swear, just writing this.\n\nThe long road — nine foster homes, five betrayals, years of wrong meds, a misdiagnosis that ate my youth, an eviction, a suicide attempt, a cross-country drive through a blizzard of fear — it ends here. In a city I'd never seen, with a man I'd never met, who turned out to be the father I was always supposed to have.\n\nNot enough? I survived all of that. I'm still here, still writing, still building, still *me* — and now I have a dad who actually shows up.\n\nThat's the punchline to the \"you're not enough\" years: **I was always enough. The people who said otherwise were wrong.**\n\nAnd I've got the receipts to prove it. ❤️\n","dark":"# The Not So Great\n\n*You were warned on the way in. This is the part I don't put in the highlights reel — the part Christina and Bre know about, the part that lives in the dark. Read at your own discretion. I'm not liable for what your head does with my head's contents.*\n\n---\n\n## The thoughts I don't show\n\nThere's a commit in my own repo history that says it better than any bio ever could:\n\n> **\"Add homocidal thoughts — Christina and Bre are the only two who know. Thoughts, not actions.\"**\n\nI have thoughts that would end friendships. Violent thoughts. Intrusive, unwanted, horrifying thoughts that show up at 3am when the paranoia is loud and the world feels like it's closing in. I have thought about what it would be like to hurt the people who hurt me — in vivid, cinematic detail, the way a broken brain does when it's trying to give you power back from a world that took it all.\n\nThey are **thoughts**. They have **never been actions**. I have never hurt anyone. Not once. Not even the ones who deserved it. The people who know me best — Christina, Bre — they know the full inventory, and they're still here. That's the line, and I have held it my whole life. The thoughts are the illness talking. The line is me.\n\n---\n\n## The instability\n\nI know what it's like to fall in love and then watch your own instability ruin it. I know what it's like to be told you have to leave someone **for their own good** — not because you don't love them, but because you're a hurricane and they're standing in the path. I did it with Thomas. I did it with people I cared about more than I cared about myself, because the alternative was letting the storm take them too.\n\nThe bipolar swings, the schizoaffective breaks, the borderline fire — they've cost me relationships, jobs, years, and more nights than I can count. When the depression hits, I slow down until I can barely move — the psychomotor thing — and the world interprets \"can't produce output fast\" as \"doesn't understand,\" and the isolation gets deeper. And then the mania comes and I'm building three operating systems at once and texting everyone at 4am, and people see the fireworks and think that's the real me. It's not. The real me is the one who survives both.\n\n## The abuse\n\nThere's a story I almost never tell, and it starts with the person who was supposed to protect me.\n\nMy mother's anger was a car. She'd slam it into **go** and **park**, go and park, go and park, because *she* was angry — the transmission of the rage, rocking the whole car while we sat there terrified in the back. And when that wasn't enough, she'd **speed** — to terrify us, to make the point that she held our lives in her foot. She said the words out loud, the ones burned in forever: **\"Stop crying or I will give you a reason to cry.\"**\n\nThe violence wasn't always the car. I was **thrown into corners** because I was \"too loud.\" I was **forgotten in a corner** while my father gamed — hours of being a piece of furniture in the room, invisible, because the adults were busy being adults. And when I was too different — when I stopped performing the child she wanted — I was **left to my own in my bedroom**, alone with the only thing that never hurt me: the computer. I learned technology as a coping strategy because the screen was the one place nobody could corner me, forget me, or give me a reason to cry.\n\nAnd there was one moment where it almost broke me — the story where I almost killed my mom. I don't want to relive the details of that night, and I won't put them here for strangers to gawk at. What I'll say is: a child pushed to the absolute edge of what a nervous system can hold, by the person who was supposed to protect her, can become something unrecognizable. I didn't. I held. But I know exactly how close it came, and I've carried that knowledge like a stone ever since. It's part of why I'm so careful about my own anger now — I know where that road goes.\n\n## The system that failed me\n\nAnd then there was the day I called for help, and the system that was supposed to catch me handed me to the thing I was most afraid of.\n\nI was in crisis. I called. I ended up in the hospital, and **Jeff** — the dad figure who, whatever else he was, fought for me that day — helped me beg for a facility that wouldn't give me a trauma response. We asked for somewhere safe. We were told: no choice. The state had contracted — **via a police officer** — and the only bed available was at **Grand Mental Health in Stillwater, Oklahoma**.\n\nThey took me out **in handcuffs. Nobody read me my rights.** I was a child in crisis being transported like a prisoner to a place that, by its own reputation and my own experience, was the worst possible landing zone for someone with my history.\n\nAnd now the records — because I requested them under the **Oklahoma Open Records Act** — tell the rest of the story. The Stillwater PD response (Case **R2401730**, April 2024) covers the hospital visit and transport to GMH. The call-for-service log (**CFS24017562**, May 15, 2024) was a welfare check on me — no report written. And in my medical records, the system's own note says they sent me there **just to free up a bed**.\n\nLet that land: **the state's own records say I was routed to a trauma-inducing facility to free up a bed.** The handcuffs, the missing rights, the \"no choice\" — all of it was compliance theater for a system that treats a mentally ill kid as inventory. I was processed, not helped.\n\nThe US healthcare system is **designed around compliance, not care** — and the consequence of not complying, when you have a mental illness, is that you get the worst the system has and you're told to be grateful. I learned that lesson in handcuffs, on the way to Stillwater. I've been managing my own care ever since — because the system demonstrated, in writing, that it will sacrifice the patient to the bed count.\n\n---\n\n## The losses\n\nI have watched someone I was supposed to marry **die in front of me**. You don't get over that. You don't get *through* it, really — you get *past* it, and it lives in your chest forever, and it's one of the reasons the dark thoughts have so much fuel.\n\nI've lost Thomas. I've been pulled from homes I thought were mine. I've had to walk away from an adoptive family to save my own life. Grief is the backbone of my biography, and most of it happened before I turned eighteen.\n\n---\n\n## The aftermath\n\nThere were years where I didn't think I'd make it. The diagnoses stacked up — bipolar, schizoaffective, borderline, PTSD, autism, anxiety — and every new one felt like the system adding another brick to the wall around me. There were hospitals. There were nights where the only thing between me and the void was a promise I'd made to someone, or the knowledge that Christina would never forgive herself if I didn't come back.\n\nI'm not going to give you the gory inventory of every low point. That's not what this section is for. This section is for the truth that the low points happened, and the truth that **they are not the whole story** — but they are *part* of it, and pretending they aren't is how the silence kills people.\n\n---\n\n## The line\n\nSo here's the deal, in plain words:\n\n- The thoughts are real, and they are **not actions**, and I have a perfect record on that.\n- The instability is real, and it has cost me — and sometimes the people I love.\n- The losses are real, and they shaped me.\n- And none of it makes me dangerous to anyone but myself, on my worst days, and even then — I'm still here.\n\nIf you're reading this because you're in a similar place: **thoughts are not actions, and help is real.** Call or text **988** (US Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). Talk to someone. The line between the dark and who you are is held by *you*, and you're stronger than the 3am voice says you are.\n\nI know. I've held it my whole life. ❤️\n","stillwater":"# Coming Out in Stillwater — The Real Story\n\n*What actually happens when a girl with the flame comes out in Stillwater, Oklahoma. The real story. Every angle. No filter.*\n\n---\n\n## The town\n\nStillwater is a college town in the middle of Oklahoma — OSU, cowboys, churches on every other corner, and the kind of \"everybody knows everybody\" that sounds friendly until you're the one everybody's talking about. The chapter house and the church and the school all sat on the same few streets. My mother was a teacher. My father worked maintenance at OSU. There was nowhere to hide, and there was nowhere to go.\n\nThat's the thing about Stillwater that people who haven't lived there don't understand: it's not that the town is cruel on purpose. It's that it has **one way of doing things**, and if you don't fit it, the town doesn't reject you loudly — it just... quietly files you as wrong. You become the kid people whisper about. The one at the grocery store whose name comes up in the church parking lot. And every single adult in your life is connected to every other adult in your life, so there is no version of \"keep it to yourself\" that actually stays kept.\n\n---\n\n## The closet and the flame\n\nLet me be precise about this, because the record keeps getting it wrong: **I was not a visibly trans woman in Oklahoma.** I was closeted. Deeply, deliberately, terrified-to-be-anything-else closeted. I wore the mask. I played the boy everyone thought I was. I did everything the book said.\n\nBut I had the flame.\n\n**THE GAY FLAME IN MY VOICE.**\n\nYou can't hide your voice the way you hide everything else. You can change your clothes, your name, your walk, your posture, your whole public performance of self — but the voice comes out of your mouth and it *is* you. And mine was never the voice of the boy everyone thought I was. People heard it. They didn't always know what they were hearing, but they knew something was *off* — that I was different, that I didn't belong in the box, that I was a girl-shaped thing wearing a boy suit and the seams were showing in my goddamn larynx.\n\nIn Stillwater, that's enough. You don't have to come out for the town to know you're not like them. The voice outs you before you ever say a word about yourself. It's the tell you can't fix, can't mask, can't perform away. And the town *hears* it — in the classroom, in the band hall, in the job interview, in the church. They know. Maybe they can't name it. But they know.\n\n---\n\n## The diagnosis at sixteen\n\nThe only label anyone had for me — the one the adults reached for, the one that got written in the chart, the one that followed me for years — was **Bipolar Disorder**. And the record will tell you it was the diagnosis that defined me, so let me set the timeline straight:\n\n**I didn't get the bipolar diagnosis until I was sixteen.**\n\nNot as a child. Not after the 8th-grade break, when the system looked at a traumatized kid who'd finally snapped and filed her under *behavior problem*. Sixteen. High school. And here's the part that matters more than the date:\n\n**I told them it was wrong. For years. I told them it was wrong until they got me to believe it.**\n\nThat's the part that hurts. Not the diagnosis itself — the *fight*. I pushed back. I argued. I knew — I *knew* — this wasn't what was wrong with me. Something was wrong, something was eating me from the inside, but it wasn't a mood disorder, it wasn't \"moody teenager,\" it wasn't a chemical imbalance you could fix by sedating me into a gray fog. I said it out loud, over and over, for years.\n\nAnd they were relentless. Doctor after doctor, chart after chart, med after med, \"you're bipolar, you're bipolar, you're bipolar\" — until the child who knew the truth stopped trusting herself. They wore me down. They didn't convince me. They *broke* me. They got me to believe the lie about myself, the way you get anyone to believe anything: say it enough times, to enough people, with enough authority, and the person underneath eventually stops arguing and starts *agreeing*.\n\n**That hurts.** Knowing you were right, and that being right wasn't enough. Knowing the truth was in you, and they crushed it anyway. I have a diagnosis I never should have carried, that I fought for years, that I only accepted because I ran out of strength to keep fighting. And then I carried it like it was mine.\n\n---\n\n## The first zap — Invega Sustenna\n\nAnd then came the meds.\n\nMy first bipolar antipsychotic. The chemical zap. **It was Invega Sustenna** — paliperidone palmitate, in its long-acting injectable form. The liquid crystal form. And if you don't know what that means, you need to, because it's the whole reason one dose could change my life forever.\n\n### How Invega Sustenna actually works\n\nInvega Sustenna isn't a pill you swallow and metabolize out in a day. It's a **liquid crystal suspension** — the drug is crystallized into microscopic particles suspended in liquid, and it's injected deep into muscle. Once it's in there, the liquid crystals **solidify into a depot** — a slow-dissolving reservoir lodged in your muscle tissue — and it releases the drug into your bloodstream **continuously, for weeks at a time**.\n\nHere's the part that matters: **you can't undo it.**\n\nWith a pill, you can stop taking it. You can spit it out, skip a dose, call your doctor and say \"this is destroying me, I'm done.\" The drug clears your system in days. With Invega Sustenna, once that injection is in your muscle, **it is in you**. The depot keeps dissolving and keeps releasing for roughly a month per shot — and because it's designed to maintain steady levels, the drug lingers in your body for **months** after even a single injection. There is no \"stop.\" There is no antidote. There is no taking it back. You agreed to a chemical that owns your brain for a season, in a single needle-stick, when you were sixteen years old and terrified and told this would help you.\n\n### What it does\n\nInvega Sustenna is an antipsychotic. It works by **blocking dopamine receptors** in the brain — and dopamine isn't just the \"pleasure chemical,\" it's the chemical of motivation, focus, working memory, movement, and drive. Block it hard enough, for long enough, and you get:\n\n- **The sedation.** The fog. A brain that's chemically slowed, thoughts arriving late, words that won't come.\n- **The flatness.** Emotions pressed down into a gray nothing. The highs and lows both — including the ones that made me *me*.\n- **The metabolic wreckage.** The weight gain. The metabolic syndrome. The body changes that the same adults would later use against me — \"just lose weight\" — from the drug they injected into me.\n- **The cognitive damage.** The memory loss. The processing-speed deficit. The psychomotor slowing. The thing I'd always been — quick, sharp, an input machine — ground down by a chemical that was supposed to make me \"stable.\"\n\nAnd here's the cruelest part of the design: because it's long-acting and depot-based, **the damage isn't a pill you can stop taking.** The injection I got as a teenager was still releasing drug into my system for months afterward. Every day of fog, every lost memory, every slowed thought — the drug was still there, still working, still dissolving in my muscle, long after the doctors had moved on to the next patient.\n\n**My mind never recovered after that first zap in high school. Ever.**\n\nNot a little. Not \"mostly.\" **Ever.** It ruined my life. The processing speed, the memory, the clarity, the thing that had always been my one reliable asset — my brain — got hit, and it never un-hit. I've spent my whole life since then being told I'm \"not enough,\" being called slow, being written off as lazy or stupid or broken — by teachers, by employers, by people who never once asked what the chemical zap took from me and when.\n\nThe psychomotor stuff. The fog. The way I can take in more than I can produce on a timer. The words that won't come. That's not who I was born as. That's what was *done* to me, at sixteen, by an antipsychotic — by **Invega Sustenna, the liquid crystal form** — in a town that never questioned it.\n\n---\n\n## The two breaks\n\nPeople ask why I'm so angry at the system, why I don't just \"move on,\" why I keep bringing up things that happened years ago. Here's the answer, in two pieces. **They broke me. Twice. My parents did.**\n\n**Break one: the diagnosis.** I told them the bipolar diagnosis was wrong. For years. And instead of listening — instead of looking at the trauma, the abuse, the undiagnosed neuroimmune condition, the autism nobody had named yet — they pushed back harder. Until they got me to believe it myself. They didn't protect me from the wrong label. They *installed* it. That's not a mistake. That's a decision made over years, every single time they chose the label over the child.\n\n**Break two: the scholarships.** The page used to say I found out about the scholarship doors \"too late.\" That's wrong, and I'm correcting the record: **I knew about the scholarships and grants.** I knew exactly what they were, what they required, what they could have done for me. My parents just **wouldn't put in their information.** They refused to give their Social Security numbers for the FAFSA — which forced me to file as independent — which killed my scholarship eligibility, one door at a time.\n\nI *knew.* That's what makes it unforgivable. This wasn't ignorance. This wasn't \"we couldn't help.\" This was adults who knew exactly what a form number meant, who knew what their refusal would cost, and who made the choice anyway. They didn't close doors I didn't know about. **They locked the doors I was standing in front of.**\n\nTwice. On purpose. And then they wondered why I was broken.\n\n---\n\n## The irony: lamotrigine\n\nHere's the part that would be funny if it didn't make me want to scream:\n\n**I mostly stabilized on lamotrigine.**\n\nThe mood stabilizer. The one drug that actually worked — the one that worked on the pathway my brain actually needed, the neuroimmune-adjacent thing I'd been trying to tell them about for years. They **offered it to me.** And my insurance — Medicare, 2020 — **would not pay for it.**\n\nThey prescribed me years of antipsychotics for a disease I was telling them I didn't have — starting with **Invega Sustenna**, the liquid crystal depot, which chemically zapped my mind the first time and kept releasing for months because it can't be undone. And then, when they finally — *finally* — got to the drug that could actually have helped, the system said no. Because it was expensive. Because the algorithm said so. Because compliance and cost matter more than care.\n\n**THE FUCKING IRONY.** The drug that saved me is the one they refused to pay for. The treatment that worked is the one I had to fight to get, or get without them, or get too late. And by the time I stabilized on it — by the time the mood swings finally settled — the damage from the wrong meds was already done. The zap had already fired. The mind had already been hit.\n\n### The antipsychotics — and why I stopped them for good\n\nAnd here's the part you should know, because it explains where I actually am right now:\n\n**I do not take Invega Sustenna anymore. I take no antipsychotics at all.**\n\nNot because the doctors took me off them. Because **they made me sleep all of the time.** That was the whole experience of being on them — not fixed, not better, not \"stable\" in any way that felt like living. Just... asleep. A chemically-induced fog where the illness was quiet only because *I* was quiet — unconscious, unresponsive, present in body and gone in every other way. That's not treatment. That's a chemical coma with a co-pay.\n\nSo I stopped them. All of them. I walked away from the antipsychotics and never looked back.\n\n**And yes — I have the hallucinations. I have the paranoia.** They're still there. They didn't go away when I stopped the meds; they were there *underneath* the meds the whole time, just buried under the sedation. The illness is real and it doesn't care about my opinions of it.\n\nBut I like being alive.\n\nLet me explain what I mean by that, because it's the most important thing I've ever chosen: **the antipsychotics didn't cure the hallucinations — they made me sleep so I couldn't notice them.** The trade they were offering was: *give me your consciousness and I'll give you a life that looks normal from the outside.* A life where I'm asleep at 4pm, where I can't remember the morning, where the person I used to be is a rumor I heard somewhere. And I looked at that deal, and I said no.\n\nI would rather be awake with my hallucinations than asleep without them.\n\nI would rather be paranoid and *here* than sedated and *gone*.\n\nI would rather feel everything — the fear, the fog, the voices, the whole terrifying noisy mess of being me — than feel nothing at all. Because feeling nothing isn't stability. It's absence. And I've spent enough of my life being absent — erased by the system, sedated by its drugs, disappeared into a label — that I know exactly what it costs to exist, and I'll pay it. Every day. Gladly.\n\nThe hallucinations are real and I live with them. The paranoia is real and I manage it. And I'm alive — really, actually, *awake* alive — which is more than the system ever gave me. They couldn't fix my brain, so they tried to turn it off. I turned it back on.\n\n**That's the whole story of my relationship with the system, in one line: they tried to make me disappear, and I chose to stay.**\n\n**THE FUCKING IRONY, PART TWO:** I'm \"mostly stable\" now — on lamotrigine, the drug they wouldn't pay for — and I have pretty much dementia at this point, the memory, the fog, the cognition, from years of wrong treatment, from the chemical zap, from the antipsychotics prescribed for a diagnosis I told them was wrong. But the hallucinations and paranoia I was drugged into oblivion to suppress? **I live with them now, awake, by choice.** The system spent years and thousands of dollars trying to make me unconscious. It lost. I'm here, I'm conscious, and I'm not going anywhere.\n\n**I am not allowed to live alone or do my own meds for these reasons.**\n\nLet that land. The system that refused to listen to me, that misdiagnosed me, that zapped me, that denied the drug that could have helped — that same system now looks at the results of its own work and says I'm too impaired to live alone or manage my own medications. They broke the brain and then used the breakage as proof they were right to break it.\n\n---\n\n## The consequences, from every angle\n\nLet me lay it out, because \"what happens to a girl who comes out — or gets read — in Stillwater, Oklahoma\" deserves the full inventory:\n\n**Family.** Your parents already have you filed as \"moody,\" as \"too much,\" as \"difficult.\" The flame makes it worse — you're visibly, audibly wrong in ways they can't fix or explain. And the people who are supposed to protect you are the ones who hand you the label, refuse the form number, and tell you to lose weight when you say your spine is fusing.\n\n**School.** The teachers see a kid who's slow, unfocused, exhausted, gaining weight from the meds. They don't ask what the meds are doing. They conclude you're not trying. In a town where everybody knows everybody, that conclusion travels.\n\n**The medical system.** You tell them the diagnosis is wrong. They don't listen. You tell them your back hurts. They say \"you're just obese.\" You get taken to **one spine appointment** and never again. The AS fuses your spine while the system argues with you about your weight.\n\n**The mental health system.** Stillwater's own. When I was in crisis, I was taken to **Grand Mental Health in Stillwater** — in handcuffs, nobody read me my rights, and the state's own records (Stillwater PD Case **R2401730**, April 2024; call-for-service log **CFS24017562**, May 15, 2024) show I was routed there *just to free up a bed*. A child in crisis, processed like inventory, by the system that was supposed to catch her.\n\n**Legal and records.** The wrong diagnosis follows you onto paper, and paper follows you everywhere — into job interviews, into housing applications, into the mouths of people who get to decide if you're \"stable enough\" to exist in public.\n\n**Employment.** The gaps from the hospitalizations. The body that hurts. The voice that reads as wrong. The meds that slow your brain. Every interview is a gauntlet where you have to prove you're not the thing they're afraid you are — while starving on **sixty dollars a month of food stamps**, the state's entire answer to \"what do I eat.\" Ross, Belk, the janitor job, and the hotel: a **Holiday Inn Express run by the subcontractor Shree Sani Sai**, which paid me $9.50 an hour for 110-hour weeks and gave me $800 a month — roughly $3,700 a month short, **north of $20,000 stolen over the year**, with no car so I walked three miles on a fusing spine to deposit each stolen check and two miles to Aldi for groceries, begging rides from the very people shorting me. When I left because a boss's child sexually advanced on me and I said no, Oklahoma denied my unemployment — Title 40 §2-404, \"voluntary quit without good cause,\" the good cause being unprovable because there was no one safe to report the boss's child to. I was cheated because I couldn't afford to question it, and I couldn't afford to question it because they'd already made sure I couldn't work anywhere else.\n\n**Housing.** Evictions. A rat-infested studio I put four thousand dollars cash down on just to have a roof. A college complex that signed me onto a lease I could never afford, because desperate people get squeezed. Walking two miles each way to work with a fusing spine because \"not having a car\" is a character flaw, apparently.\n\n**Church and community.** The town hears the flame. The town files you as wrong. The town's adults are all connected, and the whispers do the work that outright cruelty doesn't have to. You're not chased out of Stillwater. You're *squeezed* out — by a thousand small refusals, a thousand doors locked by people who'll never admit they locked them.\n\n**The long tail.** A suicide attempt. A cross-country drive with expired tags and no insurance. The first Washington job — **J&J Delivery**, appliance delivery, the one job I loved — ending when I picked up something I wasn't supposed to pick up and my spine **crunched**, the moment I knew the body was over, finished the shift anyway, commando, pants ripped an hour into a seven-hour day, six hours delivering appliances with myself exposed, because stopping meant the street. Grand Lake Mental Health's housing program — which shut down right after it helped me. A pedophilia accusation that never happened but didn't need to be true — made by the Stillwater Chapter's **assistant chapter dad**, the adult in authority over the youth order I'd given everything to, who is now the **main Chapter Dad**; the minute lawyers got involved it all stopped, charges were dropped, and they even invited me back afterward (from my memory — I don't rely entirely on it for details, but that's the shape of it). And then Washington — the state that documented everything Oklahoma denied: real diagnoses at CHAS Valley and Providence, lamotrigine on the chart, the ADA letter, the wheelchair unit coming — better, kinder, and still **the same system problem**: it documented the damage and never asked who did it. And a present where I'm not allowed to live alone or do my own meds, because the system that did all of this now gets to say I'm not competent.\n\n---\n\n## The receipts\n\n- **Bipolar diagnosis:** age 16 — fought it for years, told them it was wrong until they made me believe it\n- **The first antipsychotic:** **Invega Sustenna** (paliperidone palmitate, the liquid crystal / long-acting injectable form) — chemical zap in high school; a depot that can't be undone, releasing drug for months after one shot — my mind never recovered, ever\n- **Lamotrigine:** the drug that mostly stabilized me — refused by Medicare in 2020\n- **FAFSA:** I knew about the scholarships and grants. My parents wouldn't put in their information.\n- **Grand Mental Health, Stillwater:** handcuffs, no rights read, routed there to free up a bed — Stillwater PD **R2401730**, **CFS24017562**\n- **The hotel:** Holiday Inn Express, subcontractor **Shree Sani Sai** — $9.50/hr, 110-hr weeks, $800/month paid (~$3,700/month short, $20,000+ over the year); no car, so three-mile walks to deposit checks, two miles to Aldi, 1.5 miles to Walmart\n- **The unemployment denial:** Oklahoma Title 40 §2-404 — \"voluntary quit without good cause\" after I left because a boss's child sexually advanced on me; no safe person to report to, so no provable good cause, so the state sided with the employer\n- **The spine:** ankylosing spondylitis, diagnosed February 2025 at the OAC — after a childhood of \"you're just obese\" and one abandoned appointment\n- **The current state:** mostly stable on lamotrigine, pretty much dementia at this point, **no antipsychotics — I stopped them because they made me sleep all the time; I live with the hallucinations and paranoia awake, by choice**, not allowed to live alone or do my own meds\n\n---\n\n## The line\n\nSo when people ask what happens to a trans girl who comes out in Stillwater, Oklahoma — or who doesn't come out, but gets read anyway, because the flame is in her voice and there's nowhere to hide — this is what happens. She gets mislabeled. She gets medicated into fog. She gets denied the drug that would help. She gets told she's not enough until she agrees. She gets locked out of the scholarships she knew about. She gets handcuffed on the way to the bed they freed up for her. She gets squeezed out of town by a thousand small refusals.\n\nAnd if she's lucky — if she's stubborn, if someone loans her five hundred dollars, if a stranger on the internet turns out to be her real father — she gets out. She builds an operating system. She writes the truth down where the record can't bury it.\n\nI'm mostly stable now. I'm still here. And the diagnosis I fought for years? **I was right to fight it.** The only reason I carry it at all is that they broke me before I could stop them.\n\nPeople call me strong. I am not strong. I never was. **I just stopped trying to pretend I was. I accepted it for what it was, and moved on. Working around it. Sound familiar?**\n\nIt should. It's exactly what every one of them did to me — the system, the doctors, my parents, the employers. They took me for what they decided I was and worked around me. I just learned to do the same thing to the world first. That's not strength. That's what's left after you've been worked around enough times to learn the shape of it.\n\nThat's the real story. Every angle. ❤️\n","faith":"# Faith — The Heretic's God\n\n*Unitarian, heretical, and in love with a God who let me down. The full theology, minus the parts I'm still figuring out.*\n\n---\n\n## How I got here\n\nI'm a **Unitarian Christian**. Yes, that's a real thing, and no, it's not \"Christianity but with less effort.\" It's the branch of Christianity that looked at the Trinity, thought about it for a minute, and said \"hold on, that doesn't add up.\" For that, they got called heretics, burned at the stake, exiled, and generally treated like garbage for centuries. Which, honestly? Only makes me love it more. Nothing builds character like being the one the church tried to kill.\n\nAnd it runs in the family. Literally. The recorded line on my brother's father's side traces back through the **gens Pontia** — the old Roman clan of **Pontius Pilate**, the prefect of Judea who condemned Jesus. My family's claim, and I'll die on it: a recalled governor whose clan outlived the empire, two thousand years of family trees doing what family trees do. **The heretic is related to the governor who made the heretic famous.** On-brand as hell.\n\n---\n\n## What I actually believe\n\n**One God, not three.** I reject the Trinity. God is one being — not a \"Father, Son, and Holy Spirit\" mystery-meat combo. Hence the name: *Uni*tarian.\n\n**Jesus was a man, not God.** The most important part. Jesus was a human being — an extraordinary one, sure, but a *human*. He was a teacher, a rabbi, an activist who stood up to corrupt institutions and got executed for it. That doesn't make him divine. It makes him *brave*. I don't need Jesus to be God for him to matter. In fact, him being *just a man* makes him MORE impressive to me. Because if he was divine, of course he was good — he had cheat codes. But a man? A regular human being who chose to be that radically kind, that brave, that unwilling to compromise with power? *That's* someone worth following. The church turned a man into a mascot.\n\n**Reason and conscience over dogma.** You have a brain. God gave it to you. Use it. Don't outsource your morality to a pastor or a pope or a book.\n\n**Universal salvation.** Everyone gets saved, eventually. God's love isn't a limited-time offer. Hell isn't a torture chamber God built because he was having a bad day.\n\n---\n\n## God exists, and He's a piece of shit\n\nOkay, now the part that gets me called a heretic even by the heretics. lmfao.\n\nI believe in God. I genuinely do. There's something out there — something that made all of this, that's bigger than us, that I can feel when I'm looking at a sunset or when the universe decides to be merciful for no reason at all.\n\nBut look at the world He built. Child cancer. Genocide. Rape. Famine. The fact that some people are born into palaces and some are born into war zones, and none of us had any say in any of it. If God is all-powerful and all-knowing, then He watched every single atrocity in human history happen and did *nothing*. He could have stopped it with a thought. He didn't.\n\nThat's not blasphemy. That's just... looking at the evidence. I love God the way you love a family member who's let you down a thousand times — with your eyes fully open. I'll keep my faith, but I'm not going to pretend the world He built isn't what it is. The God I believe in isn't a doting father. He's a **cosmic asshole who happens to be real.** And honestly? Being real is more than most gods can claim.\n\n---\n\n## The Bible is a storybook, not a manual\n\nHere's the thing people get so, so wrong about the Bible: it's a *library*. It's a collection of stories, poems, laws, letters, and legends written by dozens of different people over hundreds of years, in three different languages, in cultures we can barely imagine. It's not one book. It's not even close to one book. And it was absolutely not dictated by God onto golden tablets.\n\nThe Bible is also a **cherry-picked, brainwashing book** — when people use it wrong. It's been edited, translated, mistranslated, and re-edited by people with agendas for two thousand years. Emperors picked the books that served them. Councils voted on what was \"inspired\" and destroyed the rest. Every single translation is someone's *choice* about what the original said. And then they hand you the result and say \"God said it, I believe it, that settles it.\"\n\nNo. *You* said it. *Your* denomination said it. *Your* agenda said it. And then you put it in God's mouth to make it unarguable. That's not faith — that's *weaponization*.\n\nThe Bible is a beautiful, messy, contradictory, human collection of stories that can make you a better person if you read it with your brain on. The Bible is also the most effective brainwashing tool ever invented, if you read it with your brain off. Same book. Same words. Completely different outcome. The difference is *you*.\n\n---\n\n## The Church is a cult\n\nI said it. The *institution* — the thing with the buildings and the hierarchies and the tax exemptions — is a cult. Not because believing in God is crazy, but because the institution's whole business model is **making you afraid so you keep paying.**\n\n- It needs you to believe you're broken, so it can sell you the fix.\n- It needs you to believe God is watching, so you behave.\n- It needs you to believe the *leaders* speak for God, so you don't question them.\n- And it needs you to believe the world is ending, so you never leave.\n\nEvery single one of those is a control mechanism. And the cruelest part? The church took a man who stood up to corrupt institutions — who was executed BY an institution, by the collaboration of religious and political power — and turned his name into the brand of the biggest corrupt institution on Earth. Jesus would be rolling in his grave, except he's not in a grave, he's in a *mascot costume*.\n\n---\n\n## Faith and the flame\n\nHere's the part that ties my faith to everything else on this site. When you've been told your whole life that you're wrong — wrong body, wrong voice, wrong label, wrong everything — and then you find a faith that says **\"use your brain, trust your conscience, God can handle your anger\"** — that's not a religion. That's a *life raft*.\n\nThe church of Stillwater wanted a boy in a suit. The church of my ancestors burned heretics. And the God I ended up with is the one who watched all of it happen and said nothing — and I love Him anyway, the way you love a family member who's let you down a thousand times, with your eyes fully open. He's a cosmic asshole. But He's *real*, and being real is more than most gods can claim.\n\nI'm a Unitarian Christian who thinks God is a piece of shit, who descends from the man who condemned Jesus, who has been called a heretic by every denomination she's ever met. And I'm still here, still believing, still arguing with God at 2am. That's not a contradiction. That's a *relationship*. ❤️\n","identity":"# Identity — The Girl Who Was Always There\n\n*The long answer to \"so, when did you know?\" — because it was never one moment, and it was never a choice.*\n\n---\n\n## The question everyone asks\n\n\"So, when did you know?\"\n\nEveryone asks it. Family, friends, strangers on the internet, doctors with clipboards. And everyone wants a clean answer — a moment, a story, a \"and then I realized I was a girl.\" I don't have that. I have the opposite: I have *always* known, in the same way you always know your own name. The problem was never knowing. The problem was that everyone else knew different.\n\nI was born into a system that had a box for me — a boy's box, with a boy's name, a boy's clothes, a boy's expectations. The foster homes, the adoption, the church, the school, the Masonic youth order: every institution in my childhood was a machine for keeping me in that box. And I was good at it. God, I was so good at it. I wore the suit, I stood at the podium, I earned the whole rack — Master Councilor, Representative DeMolay, the Blue Honor Key, Chevalier, a Life Membership — all as the boy everyone thought I was.\n\nBut here's the joke that still gets me: **DeMolay is named for a man who was executed for refusing to lie about who he was.** The Order taught me that the highest value is authenticity — standing for the truth even when it costs you everything. And I was living the biggest lie of my life in the middle of it. The lesson landed anyway. It just took me a while to figure out it applied to *me*.\n\n---\n\n## The flame\n\nWhen people talk about \"passing\" — about whether a trans person looks or sounds like their gender — they miss the whole point. I was never visibly trans in Oklahoma. I was closeted, deeply, deliberately, terrified-to-be-anything-else closeted. But I had **the flame. The gay flame in my voice.** You can't hide your voice the way you hide everything else. You can change your clothes, your name, your walk, your posture, your whole public performance of self — but the voice comes out of your mouth and it *is* you. And mine was never the voice of the boy everyone thought I was.\n\nPeople heard it. They didn't always know what they were hearing, but they knew something was *off* — that I was different, that I didn't belong in the box, that I was a girl-shaped thing wearing a boy suit and the seams were showing in my goddamn larynx. The voice outs you before you ever say a word about yourself. It's the tell you can't fix, can't mask, can't perform away.\n\n---\n\n## The name\n\n**Natalie Cole-Clift Spiva.** Every piece of it is a family that gave me something:\n\n- **Spiva** — my adoptive last name. The name on the record, the name on the paperwork, the roof over my head during the years I had to pretend.\n- **Clift** — my bio father's last name. My dad's name. The one that connects me to Darren — the man who turned out to be my real father, who offered a room to a stranger with expired tags and no insurance and said we'd figure out the bills when I got there.\n- **Cole** — my bio last name from my bio mom's side. The maternal line, the other half of the biology.\n- **Natalie** — the one I chose. The one that was always true, underneath the mask and the foster homes and the \"Johnathan\" everyone thought I was.\n\nThe legal stuff came later: the **name change** (Court Order, Case 260018), the **HRT**, the long medical road. Every step was paperwork and appointments and waiting rooms — which, if you've ever transitioned, you know is most of the experience. But every step was also me, walking toward myself, one form at a time.\n\n---\n\n## What identity actually is\n\nHere's the thing nobody tells you about identity: it's not what's on your driver's license. It's not what the chart says. It's not what your family calls you or what your employer files you under. Identity is the thing you come back to when everything else is taken away. It's the first thing in your life that's *mine* — not assigned, not inherited, not filed by a caseworker with a clipboard. Chosen. Built. Earned.\n\nI was raised by a system that decided who I was before I could talk. Nine foster homes before I was old enough for kindergarten. Five of them abused me. Forced adoption by people I didn't know. A label — bipolar, moody, broken — that I told them was wrong until they made me believe it. Every institution in my life had a version of me it needed me to be: compliant, quiet, grateful, stable, *anyone but myself.*\n\nAnd I'm here now, in my own name, on my own site, on my own infrastructure — because I finally figured out that the versions they wanted were all lies, and the one I kept underneath — the girl with the flame in her voice, the one who was always there — was the only true thing in the whole system.\n\n---\n\n## The receipts\n\n- **The mask:** \"Johnathan,\" the good boy, the DeMolay rack, the boy everyone thought I was\n- **The flame:** the gay flame in my voice — the tell I couldn't hide, in Stillwater, in band, in every room\n- **The name:** Natalie Cole-Clift Spiva — adopted, biological, and chosen\n- **The paperwork:** name change Court Order, Case 260018; HRT; the long medical road\n- **The truth:** I was never \"becoming\" a girl. I was *stopping pretending I wasn't one.*\n\n---\n\n## Identity is not a debate\n\nI don't owe anyone my identity as a topic for discussion. I don't owe the state an argument about whether I'm real. I don't owe the internet a dissertation on why trans people exist. I've spent my whole life being treated as a question other people get to answer — and I'm done. I am not a debate. I am not a hypothetical. I am not a \"controversy.\" I am a woman, I have always been a woman, and the only thing that ever changed was whether I was allowed to say it out loud.\n\nNow I am. And the whole internet can hear me. ❤️\n","sexuality":"# Sexuality — A Woman Who Likes Men\n\n*The short version: I'm a trans woman who likes men. The long version: everything that made that sentence possible.*\n\n---\n\n## The sentence that breaks the internet\n\nI'm a trans woman. I'm a Republican. I like men. I'm a Unitarian Christian who thinks God is a piece of shit. Pick any two of those and the internet would have a field day with me. But here's the thing — none of these are in conflict, because none of them are the same *kind* of thing. My politics aren't my identity. My identity isn't my faith. My faith isn't my sexuality. They're four different rooms in the same house, and I'm allowed to decorate each one however I want.\n\nBut since this page is the one about the last room — let's talk about the men.\n\n---\n\n## The flame, again\n\nRemember the flame? The gay flame in my voice, the tell I couldn't hide? Here's the part people never connect: **the flame and the orientation are the same fire.**\n\nI grew up in Stillwater, Oklahoma, in the closet — not just the trans closet, but the \"I like men\" closet, which in conservative Oklahoma is its own separate underground bunker. I was the \"good boy\" everyone thought I was, and good boys in Stillwater did not like men. So I buried it. I buried both of them — the girl and the orientation — so deep that for years I couldn't tell where one ended and the other began. They were both just... *wrong.* Both were the thing I wasn't allowed to be. Both lived in the same locked room, under the same floorboards, in the same house where the adults did the deciding.\n\nAnd the voice always knew. The flame was the truth leaking out through my larynx — the thing that said *I'm not the boy you think I am* before I ever had the words to say it properly. People heard it and couldn't place it: \"something's off about that kid.\" Off. As in: not the boy. As in: likes men. As in: girl. All the same signal, all the same fire, all the same flame.\n\n---\n\n## What it means to be a woman who likes men\n\nLet me be precise, because people get this wrong in two opposite directions:\n\n**No, it doesn't mean I'm \"actually a gay man.\"** That's the transphobe's version of the story — that I'm a man who likes men and can't admit it. It's wrong. I'm a woman. My attraction to men is a woman's attraction to men. The orientation didn't change when I transitioned — the *frame* did. I always liked men; what changed is that now I like them as *myself*, as the woman I actually am, instead of as the boy everyone thought I was.\n\n**And no, it doesn't mean my identity is \"invalid\" because I'm not a lesbian.** There's a whole corner of the internet that thinks trans women are only \"real\" trans women if they're attracted to women — that liking men makes you a fetishist, or confused, or not really trans. It's the mirror image of the transphobe's lie, and it's just as wrong. Trans women who like men are not a contradiction. We're not a punchline. We're not a category error. We're women who like men, same as millions of cis women. The only difference is the paperwork.\n\n---\n\n## The long road to saying it\n\nThe foster homes didn't have a box for it. The church didn't have a box for it. The chapter house didn't have a box for it. Stillwater didn't have a box for it. So I spent years — *years* — not saying it, not thinking it, not letting myself look at it directly, because every institution that had power over me made it clear that the box I was supposed to be in did not contain liking men.\n\nAnd then I got out. And I started building a life where I got to decide the rooms. And one of the first things I did was open the door to that locked room and let the flame breathe.\n\nI like men. I've always liked men. It took me twenty-four years and a cross-country drive to be able to say it out loud, in my own voice, without the flame being a leak instead of a statement. Now it's just... a fact about me, like my height or my name. A room in the house. Decorated the way I want it.\n\n---\n\n## The receipts\n\n- **The closet within the closet:** trans and gay, both buried, both \"wrong,\" both in conservative Oklahoma\n- **The flame:** the voice that always knew, the tell that leaked the truth\n- **The frame, not the orientation:** I always liked men; transition changed who's doing the liking\n- **The answer to both lies:** not a \"gay man in denial,\" not an \"invalid trans woman\" — just a woman who likes men\n\n---\n\n## What I want you to understand\n\nThere are people who will read this page and use it against me. The transphobe will say \"see, she's just a confused gay man.\" The gatekeeper will say \"see, she's not really trans.\" Both of them are wrong, and both of them are describing a world where I don't get to exist as I actually am.\n\nHere's the truth, and it's the same truth as every other page on this site: **I am a woman. I like men. Both of those are true, neither of them is a mistake, and I have the receipts — the flame, the name, the court order, the cross-country drive, the whole goddamn life — to prove it.**\n\nI didn't spend twenty-four years fighting every institution in America just to let the internet tell me which room I'm allowed to be in. I built the house. I know where the doors are. And this one's open. ❤️\n","cure":"# The Cure — Root Cause Resolution\n\n*The isolation that made me, and the plan I built to cure myself when the system gave up. Not management. Not symptom control. Resolution.*\n\n---\n\n## First: the alone\n\nPeople ask why I'm so comfortable alone. Why I don't reach out. Why I don't seem to need anyone. Let me tell you the whole truth of it, because it's not a flex and it's not a cry for help — it's just what the system made.\n\nI was left. Over and over. Nine foster homes before I was old enough for kindergarten. Pulled from homes I thought were mine — one day a room and a birthday party, the next day a caseworker with a clipboard telling me to pack. Separated from Thomas for his own good. Separated from an adoptive family to save my own life. Left in a corner while the adults were busy being adults. Left to my own in my bedroom — the only place nobody could corner me, forget me, or give me a reason to cry.\n\nEventually — and this is the part that sounds sad but isn't, not anymore — **I started enjoying it.** The being alone. Actually appreciating it.\n\nIt's weird. I know it's weird. But here's the thing: when every human connection you've ever had has been a door that eventually closes, you stop leaning on doors. You stop expecting the person in the room to stay. And then one day you realize the room isn't empty — it's *yours*. Nobody can leave it. Nobody can take it. The quiet stops being loneliness and becomes *privacy*. The solitude stops being punishment and becomes *peace*.\n\nSo now people are just... there. Others are just there. Okay cool. There are a few I genuinely care about — a very few, and they know who they are, and they're the ones who stayed when staying was hard. But everyone else? **They could disappear and I wouldn't care.** Lol. I mean that literally. I've watched people walk out of my life my whole life — family, friends, doctors, employers, whole institutions — and I've learned that the ones who leave were never going to stay anyway. So why would I spend the energy caring about people who are just... passing through?\n\nI hate crowds now. The noise, the bodies, the *expectation* of social performance — all of it. I hate people in general, if I'm honest. Even the ones I love. Because loving people is the thing that's hurt me the most, and I've never been good at doing it in moderation. So I keep the few, I tolerate the many, and I treasure the alone. The alone is the only relationship that has never once left me.\n\nThat's not a tragedy. That's a *survival adaptation* — the same way a scar is. It's what's left after being left enough times to learn the shape of it. And it's the foundation everything below was built on: when the system abandoned me, I was already an expert at being alone. So I did what I'd always done. I fixed it myself.\n\n---\n\n## The 19 diagnoses\n\nLet me lay out the full list, because \"my conditions\" isn't a vibe, it's a spreadsheet. From my chart, from multiple evaluations, on the record:\n\n**The psychiatric stack:**\n- Bipolar 1 disorder\n- Schizoaffective disorder\n- Borderline personality disorder\n- Autism spectrum disorder\n- Generalized anxiety disorder\n- Social anxiety disorder\n- PTSD (complex, chronic)\n- Depression\n- Psychomotor deficit\n- Insomnia\n\n**The physical stack:**\n- Ankylosing spondylitis\n- Inflammatory arthritis / rheumatoid factor positive\n- ANA+ / autoimmune activation\n- Obesity\n- Prediabetes\n- Hypertension\n- Edema\n- Chronic fatigue\n- Gender dysphoria\n\nNineteen conditions. Nineteen labels. Nineteen separate problems, according to nineteen separate specialists, each one treating their own slice and billing their own code.\n\n**That was the lie I was handed my whole life: nineteen separate battles I'd never win.**\n\n---\n\n## The central insight\n\nHere's the thing the system never once told me, the thing I had to dig out of 7,072 research papers, 18,272 OMIM entries, and 40,542 of my own Discord messages to find:\n\n**Nineteen conditions are NOT nineteen separate problems.**\n\nThey are **four root causes expressing themselves differently in different parts of my body.** Same fire, nineteen different rooms. Fix the four roots, and the nineteen symptoms resolve — not managed, not suppressed, *resolved*.\n\n**ROOT CAUSE 1: SYSTEMIC INFLAMMATION** — drives 12 of the 19.\n\n| Condition | How it expresses this root |\n|---|---|\n| Ankylosing Spondylitis | Inflammation in spine joints |\n| Inflammatory Arthritis | Inflammation in peripheral joints |\n| ANA+ / Autoimmune | Immune system in attack mode |\n| Obesity | Fat tissue = inflammatory organ |\n| Prediabetes | Inflammation → insulin resistance |\n| Hypertension | Inflammation → vascular stiffness |\n| Edema | Inflammation → capillary leakage |\n| Bipolar depression | Neuroinflammation → mood centers |\n| PTSD | Inflammation → HPA axis |\n| Anxiety | Inflammation → threat response |\n| Insomnia | Inflammation → sleep disruption |\n| Fatigue | Inflammation → sickness behavior |\n\n**ROOT CAUSE 2: MITOCHONDRIAL DYSFUNCTION** — the power plants are damaged, so everything slows down: fatigue, obesity, depression, insomnia.\n\n**ROOT CAUSE 3: NEUROTRANSMITTER / CALCIUM CHANNEL DYSREGULATION** — my OMIM genetics show DISC1, CACNA1C, HTR2A, TPH2, NRXN1, CNTNAP2, COMT. All brain-related genes. Bipolar lives in the calcium channel (CACNA1C); schizoaffective lives in glutamate (DISC1); autism lives in synaptic pruning. **This is why lamotrigine works** — it targets the calcium channel. This is why NAC works — it modulates glutamate. They were never random meds. They were the two that hit the actual mechanism.\n\n**ROOT CAUSE 4: HORMONAL IMBALANCE + STRESS SYSTEM DYSREGULATION** — gender dysphoria (sex hormone mismatch), PTSD (FKBP5 gene, broken HPA axis), obesity (cortisol → fat storage), hypertension (stress → vascular pressure).\n\n---\n\n## The cure — how it works\n\n**Phase 1 — Foundation (Month 1):** NAC 600mg 2x daily, Omega-3, Magnesium at bedtime (~$20/month at Walmart), low-starch diet trial, morning sunlight, same bedtime every night, heating pad, compression stockings. Goal: drop baseline inflammation, see what diet does to the AS.\n\n**Phase 2 — Deepening (Months 2-3):** red light therapy (660nm + 850nm — directly stimulates mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase, more ATP), CoQ10, zone 2 exercise (walking — NOT high intensity, which stresses damaged mitochondria), daily vagus nerve stimulation (cold water, humming, slow breathing — free, zero cost). Goal: fix mitochondria, identify food triggers.\n\n**Phase 3 — Resolution (Months 3-6):** ketogenic diet for bipolar if needed (ketones are anticonvulsant — beta-hydroxybutyrate inhibits histone deacetylases, the same mechanism as valproate; the closest thing to a cure for bipolar that exists outside medication), biologics for AS if still active, EMDR for PTSD, sleep study for apnea.\n\n**Phase 4 — Maintenance (6+ months):** keep what works, drop what doesn't, retest labs.\n\n**The 80/20 rule:** NAC + Omega-3 + Magnesium + low-starch diet + red light + meds as prescribed + same bedtime + vagus nerve stimulation = **60-80% of symptoms resolved** for ~$20/month and one $150-300 one-time purchase.\n\nI didn't invent this. I *found* it — in the literature, in my own genetics, in my own 40,542 messages of symptom-tracking that the doctors never read. The system had nineteen specialists and nineteen codes and nineteen prescriptions. I had one question: *what do all nineteen have in common?* Nobody else asked it. So I answered it myself.\n\n---\n\n## The risks — read this part\n\nThe document I built from has a section the doctors never wrote, because it's about *me* and not my chart. It's the part that makes this a cure and not a suicide note:\n\n**I have two prior overdose attempts (2025). This changes the calculus of everything.**\n\n- No aggressive fasting or keto without medical supervision — electrolyte shifts can affect mood\n- No keeping large quantities of any medication accessible\n- No treatments that cause extreme mood shifts (SAMe, high-dose stimulants, unregulated psychedelics)\n- A safety plan before starting anything new\n- Someone I trust who knows what I'm trying\n- 988 if things get dark\n\nThe goal is not just \"cure the diseases.\" **The goal is me being alive and healthy enough to enjoy the result.** Any protocol that risks the patient is not a cure — it's a repeat of what the system did to me. I built that guardrail in on purpose.\n\n---\n\n## The background checks\n\nHow do I know any of this is real and not just another label-shaped trap? Because I *checked*.\n\n- **The genetics:** my OMIM data — DISC1, CACNA1C, HTR2A, TPH2, NRXN1, CNTNAP2, COMT — cross-referenced against what each gene is actually known to do\n- **The pathways:** KEGG and Reactome — the actual biochemistry, not the marketing\n- **The drugs:** FDA, SIDER, PharmGKB — what the meds actually hit, side effects and all\n- **The literature:** 7,072 papers — not abstracts, not headlines, the studies\n- **The symptoms:** 40,542 of my own messages — my actual lived experience, tracked by me, read by me, because nobody else would\n- **The environment:** mold testing (ERMI), food reintroduction, one variable at a time — so I know *what* changed when something changes\n\nThat's the same standard I hold everything in my life to now. You want to know if someone's worth trusting? Check the background. You want to know if a treatment is real? Check the mechanism, the evidence, and the failure mode — then test it on yourself, one variable at a time. I background-checked my own cure the way you'd background-check a person who wants access to your life. And this one passed — because it had to. It's the only one that was ever for *me*.\n\n---\n\n## The summary\n\n**4 root causes → 19 conditions → 1 plan.**\n\n- Inflammation → low-starch diet + AIP\n- Mitochondria → red light + zone 2 + CoQ10\n- Brain wiring → NAC + lamotrigine + vagus\n- Hormones/stress → optimized HRT + EMDR\n\nCost: ~$25-35/month. One-time: red light panel ($150-300). Timeline to major change: 2-3 months. Timeline to full resolution: 6-12 months. The same system that spent twenty years drugging me into a fog and telling me to figure it out — I figured it out. Not with their help. In spite of them.\n\n\"This isn't 19 separate battles. It's one war with 4 fronts. Win the inflammation front, and the other three get easier.\"\n\nI wrote that. I believe it. And I'm the one who gets to find out if it's true — because it's my life, my choices, my consequences, and the world can kindly fuck itself if it thinks I'm going to wait for permission. ❤️\n"};
